gue records and access permissions.", "source": "page 1", "display": "The system will collect resource metadata, normalize titles and keywords, and rank results using deterministic relevance rules. A role-based web interface will allow students to search resources while administrators manage catalogue records and access permissions.", "source_page": 1}, "table_count": 0, "figure_count": 0, "font_size_pt": 8.3, "line_spacing": 0.36, "top_margin_mm": 70.68, "other_sections": [{"value": "[PAGE 1]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "AI-Assisted Smart Library Recommendation System", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Dummy One-Page Proposal | Proposal Extraction Demo 2", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}], "page_numbering": false, "required_fields": ["title", "purpose"], "right_margin_mm": 78.52, "bottom_margin_mm": 223.15, "detected_headings": [{"name": "1. Build a searchable digital library catalogue. 2. Recommend relevant resources using topic and keyword", "page": 1}], "expected_outcomes": {"value": "A faster and more organized academic resource discovery experience, improved research support for students, and a maintainable digital catalogue for university departments. Keywords: smart library, academic search, resource recommendation, student research, digital catalogue", "source": "page 1", "display": "A faster and more organized academic resource discovery experience, improved research support for students, and a maintainable digital catalogue for university departments. Keywords: smart library, academic search, resource recommendation, student research, digital catalogue", "source_page": 1}, "proposed_solution": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "missing_required_fields": []}	1256	\N	2026-11-05 12:18:05.053142+00	2026-11-05 12:18:05.053142+00	\N
3	c0e71c67-e0b5-4e73-bd98-36ec901b8b58	34847	LONG	long	1	SUBMITTED	COMPLETED	Interactive Projection Mapping Spatialvision.pdf	application/pdf	400171	702ac0ba1330c43deff5c41014215344330380511c92255a28e26c68a555f0b8	17	module-3\\submissions\\c0e71c67-e0b5-4e73-bd98-36ec901b8b58\\v1-Interactive_Projection_Mapping_Spatialvision.pdf	[PAGE 1]\nFinal Year Project Long Proposal\nInteractive Projection Mapping\nFramework Using Computer Vision\nand Spatial Tracking\nProduct Name: SpatialVision\nSyed Ali Tarikh (23108189)\nZahra Tul Hussan Syed (23108195)\nM. Anas Bhatti (23108243)\nSupervised by: Mr. Saad Irfan Khan\nDepartment of Robotics & Artificial Intelligence\nFaculty of Computing and Engineering Sciences\nSZABIST University\nIslamabad, Pakistan\nFall 2026\n[PAGE 2]\nRevision History\nCompiled By Checked By Date Description V ersion\nM Anas Bhatti Mr. Saad Irfan Khan 24-Sept-2026 Section 1 1.0\nZahra Tul Hussan Syed Mr. Saad Irfan Khan 01-Sept-2026 Section 2 1.1\nSyed Ali Tarikh Mr. Saad Irfan Khan 05-Oct-2026 Section 3 1.2\n[PAGE 3]\n1. Project Stream\n✓ Mobile App\n2. Introduction\nIntroduction In recent years, the convergence of display technologies and spatial com-\nputing has redefined how digital media interacts with the physical world. Traditional\nvisual display systems rely heavily on flat, two-dimensional screens or headsets to present\naugmented content, restricting user engagement within fixed rectangular boundaries [1].\nSpatial Augmented Reality (SAR), widely known as projection mapping, overcomes these\nphysical constraints by utilizing everyday physical objects as dynamic display surfaces [2].\nBy precise alignment of visual projections over complex 3D surfaces, projection mapping\nmerges digital graphics seamlessly with tangible geometry, unlocking immersive experi-\nences across digital art, retail product showcases, medical visualization, and interactive\neducation.\nDespite its growing popularity, conventional projection mapping setups remain largely\nstatic and non-interactive. Traditional systems require pre-calculated calibration and\nmanual alignment tailored strictly to fixed projector-object positions [3]. Any slight\nmovement or shift in the physical object breaks the visual alignment, leading to noticeable\ndistortion and visual artifacts. Furthermore, most existing setups lack real-time user\nresponsiveness, treating the viewer merely as a passive observer rather than an active\nparticipant. Relying on external handheld controllers or physical touch panels to interact\nwith projected content further disrupts the natural, touchless interaction model that\nspatial displays inherently promise.\nBy integrating continuous spatial tracking with real-time pose estimation and touch-\nless gesture interaction, this project advances SAR beyond static installations into adap-\ntive, touchless interactive environments. Eliminating the need for manual re-calibration\nwhen objects shift significantly reduces technical overhead in interactive displays. Beyond\nits immediate application as a functional prototype, this work aligns with broader indus-\ntry shifts toward ambient computing, spatial human-computer interaction, and touchless\ndisplay interfaces across interactive retail, smart educational tools, and immersive simu-\nlation environments.\n3. Project Description\nTraditional projection mapping systems are static, requiring manual calibration that\nbreaks whenever an object moves [3]. This project solves that problem by building an\n[PAGE 4]\ninteractive, touchless projection system that adapts visuals to object movement in real\ntime. The main goal is to track physical objects as they shift or rotate and dynamically\nwarp projected graphics so they stay perfectly aligned. To make interaction natural, the\nsystem uses computer vision to recognize hand gestures, letting users control projected\ncontent without touchscreens or handheld controllers. The final outcome is a working\nprototype for rigid 3D objects [4] in indoor settings, providing an intuitive interface for\ninteractive retail, smart classrooms, and public exhibits.\nThe underlying pipeline processes video input using Python and OpenCV to detect\nsurface boundaries and track spatial orientation in 3D space [4]. Estimated pose co-\nordinates (x, y, zposition and rotation) feed directly into a rendering pipeline built in\nUnity, which warps and transforms the graphics before projecting them back onto the\nobject. In parallel, MediaPipe analyzes camera frames to detect hand landmarks and\ntrack gestures, translating movements into control commands like swapping projected\nanimations or selecting menu items [5]. A calibrated camera-projector setup closes the\nfeedback loop, continuously matching real-time computer vision tracking with physical\nprojection output.\nBy combining low-latency tracking algorithms with touchless gesture interaction, this\nproject demonstrates how spatial computing can operate seamlessly without expensive\nheadset hardware or rigid, permanent physical mountings. The system bridges the gap\nbetween digital content and physical physical geometry, reducing the setup overhead\nusually required for interactive spatial displays. Ultimately, this approach establishes\na scalable baseline for future spatial augmented reality applications, paving the way\nfor adaptive interfaces in indoor advertising, immersive educational tools, and smart\nenvironments.\n4. Real World Problem\nIn modern industries such as retail showcases, museum exhibitions, digital education,\nand architectural visualization, spatial computing and Spatial Augmented Reality (SAR)\nare increasingly relied upon to create engaging customer and visitor experiences. Tra-\nditional display technologies depend heavily on flat 2D screens or personal headsets like\nVR/AR goggles. However, flat displays constrain digital media within fixed rectangular\nborders, while headsets isolate users from one another, preventing shared social interac-\ntions. Projection mapping overcomes these physical barriers by transforming everyday\nphysical objects into dynamic display surfaces, merging digital imagery directly onto\ntangible 3D geometry without requiring users to wear cumbersome hardware [6].\nDespite its growing adoption, traditional projection mapping suffers from significant\noperational limitations that hinder its widespread deployment in real-world environments.\nConventional setups rely on pre-calculated geometric alignments tailored strictly to fixed\n[PAGE 5]\nprojector and object positions [3]. If a target object is moved, rotated, or accidentally\nbumped, the projected content immediately misaligns, leading to noticeable visual dis-\ntortion and breaking the immersive experience. Re-aligning the projection manually\nrequires specialized technical expertise and substantial downtime, making existing setups\nimpractical for dynamic environments like retail spaces, museum exhibits, or interactive\nclassrooms where physical layout changes occur frequently [7].\nFurthermore, most existing projection systems lack real-time user responsiveness,\ntreating the viewer merely as a passive observer. When interaction is offered, it typically\ndepends on physical touch surfaces, touch sensors, or handheld controllers. In public or\nshared environments, physical contact interfaces raise hygiene concerns, suffer from me-\nchanical wear and tear, and disrupt the natural, touchless interaction model that spatial\ndisplays inherently promise. Without continuous spatial tracking and intuitive touchless\ncontrol, spatial augmented reality remains confined to costly, static installations, limiting\nuser engagement and imposing high technical overhead on organizations.\n5. Problem Statement\nSpatial Augmented Reality (SAR) has transformed interactive media by projecting\nvisual content directly onto physical 3D surfaces, eliminating the need for headsets or flat\nscreens [1]. However, its effectiveness in dynamic environments—such as retail displays,\nmuseums, and educational spaces—is severely restricted by traditional calibration meth-\nods. When target objects move, rotate, or shift positions, the projected visuals instantly\nmisalign, resulting in severe visual distortion, broken immersion, and high manual setup\noverhead for non-technical users.\nConventional projection mapping systems lack automated real-time spatial tracking\nand touchless user interaction, causing immediate visual alignment failures whenever\ntarget objects shift position and forcing reliance on physical controllers or touch panels.\nThis static nature limits spatial displays to rigid, non-interactive installations that require\nfrequent technical intervention. Without continuous computer vision tracking, existing\nsetups fail to maintain real-time alignment or support natural, touchless interaction for\ndynamic user environments [8].\nFailing to address these tracking and interaction bottlenecks restricts SAR from adopt-\ning intuitive, ambient computing models across public and commercial domains. Relying\non physical touch interfaces or manual recalibration increases operational maintenance,\ncreates hygiene concerns in public spaces, and degrades user experience. Developing a\nself-aligning, gesture-controlled projection system is essential to bridge the gap between\nstatic projection and adaptive, touchless spatial interaction.\n[PAGE 6]\n6. Names of existing similar FYPs (if repeated)\nN/A.\n7. New Functionalities (main) to be added Features\nN/A.\n8. Application Review\nCommercial projection mapping software like HeavyM, MadMapper, and Resolume\nArena are widely used for visual setups in stage performances, digital art installations,\nand commercial exhibits. HeavyM and MadMapper focus on geometric warping, grid\nmapping, and edge blending for visual designers and event technicians. Resolume Arena,\non the other hand, is built primarily for live video mixing and performance setups. These\ntools offer rich visual effect libraries, multi-projector support, and timeline controls that\nallow operators to manually align digital content onto physical 3D structures with high\ngraphical quality.\nDespite their rendering capabilities, current commercial tools struggle in dynamic\nenvironments because they rely entirely on static, manual setup. If a physical object\nshifts, rotates, or gets bumped, the software cannot adjust the graphics on its own. The\nprojected image immediately distorts until a technician manually recalibrates the grid.\nAdditionally, these platforms do not include built-in computer vision for touchless inter-\naction. Users must rely on physical controllers, keyboard shortcuts, or MIDI equipment\nto change content, creating technical friction for everyday users in interactive retail or\neducational spaces.\nThe system developed in this project solves these problems by combining real time\nspatial tracking and camera based gesture recognition directly into the rendering pipeline.\nBy using continuous camera input to track object movement and hand gestures, the\napplication automatically warps the projected graphics to match physical movement while\nenabling touchless control.\n8.1 SurfaBeam [9]\nSurfaBeam Pro is a cross-platform projection mapping app that lets creators warp,\nmask, and align video onto physical surfaces straight from a mobile device or PC no bulky\nmedia servers or expensive setups required.\nThe key characteristics of the application are listed below.\n•Touch-Optimized Real-Time Engine: Leverages on-device GPU processing so you\ncan warp meshes, pin corners, and control timelines directly with touch gestures\n[PAGE 7]\nwithout lag.\n•Universal Interface Across Devices: Delivers the exact same layout, performance,\nand workflow whether you run it on iOS, Android, macOS, or Windows\n•100% Offline and Private: Runs entirely on local hardware with zero cloud render-\ning, accounts, or background data tracking.\nThe limitations of the application are listed below.\n•No Automated Cloud Syncing: Projects don’t sync between platforms automati-\ncally, so files have to be moved manually between devices.\n•Gated Free Tier: The trial mode adds a watermark, displays a launcher prompt,\nand locks project saving and exporting behind the Pro license.\n•Bandwidth and Cable Dependency: Output stability relies heavily on a high-quality\ndisplay adapter (USB-C/HDMI) or a stable 5GHz Wi-Fi link to prevent frame drops\nduring live projection.\n8.2 Lazy Lightning [10]\nLazy Lighting (by Audity Apps LLC) is a mobile-first projection mapping application\nfor iOS and Android. It lets creators, VJs, and DIY decorators shape, warp, and align\nvideo effects or personal media onto real-world objects directly from a phone or tablet.\nThe key characteristics of the application are listed below.\n•Audio and MIDI Reactive Controls: Features over 100 generated live visuals that\ndynamically sync to tempo, microphone input, or MIDI controllers.\n•Comprehensive Mapping Toolkit: Includes touch-native corner warping, masks,\ncurved shapes, box mapping, and drawing tools directly on mobile devices.\n•Standalone ”Lazy Lighting Link” Offloading: Allows users to export and transfer\ncompleted shows to dedicated TV/projector receiver apps to play without keeping\nthe phone connected.\nThe limitations of the application are listed below.\n•Single-Projector Focus: Built specifically for simple single-display setups, lacking\nmulti-projector edge blending and advanced spatial calibration required for large\narchitectural mapping.\n•Pay walled Feature Set: Key mapping features, show export capabilities, and ad-\nvanced visual templates are locked behind a paid Pro subscription tier.\n[PAGE 8]\n8.3 Pro Mapper [11]\nPro Mapper is an iOS-exclusive mobile projection mapping solution that turns any\niPhone or iPad into an instant spatial visual studio. Powered by Apple’s Metal GPU\nengine, it allows event creators, visual artists, and performers to warp meshes, trigger\naudio-reactive effects, and drive interactive 3D portals on physical surfaces without bring-\ning a laptop.\nThe key characteristics of the application are listed below.\n•Advanced Metal GPU Performance: Powered by Apple’s Metal rendering engine\nto deliver real-time, touch-reactive fluid visualizers and low-latency multi-surface\nwarping.\n•Interactive FX and Head-Tracking Portals: Includes unique creative capabilities\nsuch as head-tracked 3D projection boxes, live person segmentation/glowing out-\nlines, and animated typography.\n•Multi-Layer Timeline Control: Features a unified global timeline with individual\nlanes, clip layering, stroke-by-stroke drawing animations, and direct video export\nto Photos.connected.\nThe limitations of the application are listed below.\n•Apple Ecosystem Exclusivity: Available only on iOS, iPadOS, and macOS (M-\nseries), completely excluding Android and Windows operating systems.\n•Single-Display Output Constraints: Lacks multi-projector edge blending and ad-\nvanced spatial calibration required for large-scale architectural mapping installa-\ntions.\n8.4 Holo Mapper [12]\nA node-based visual programming environment built for custom, highly interactive\ninstallations driven by live sensors, cameras, and audio feeds.\nThe key characteristics of the application are listed below.\n•Subscription-Free Flat-Rate Pricing: Available as a one-time purchase on Windows\nand SteamOS without recurring monthly subscription fees.\n•Multi-Format Local Playback: Easily loads and warps video files, animated GIFs,\nimage loops, and direct live capture feeds onto physical targets.\n•Lightweight Desktop Engine: Designed for fast deployment on standard PCs and\nhandheld SteamOS devices with low CPU/GPU resource overhead.\n[PAGE 9]\nThe limitations of the application are listed below.\n•No Multi-Projector Edge Blending: Built for single-display mapping setups, making\nit unsuitable for complex multi-projector architectural displays.\n•Basic Toolset: Lacks advanced VJ features like audio-reactive effect generators,\nDMX/LED lighting integration, and complex timeline sequencing.\n8.5Product: SpatialVision (Proposed)\nThis project develops a self-aligning projection mapping system using computer vision\nto track object movements in real time.\nAutomatically warps projected graphics onto shifted 3D surfaces and detects hand\ngestures to eliminate manual recalibration while enabling touchless control.\nThe key characteristics of the application are listed below.\n•Real-time Surface and Pose Tracking uses computer vision to continuously monitor\n3D object movement and rotation in physical space. This allows the system to\ndynamically warp projected graphics so that they stay perfectly aligned without\nmanual recalibration.\n•Touchless Gesture Control captures user hand movements through camera feed-\nback to translate gestures into direct interactive commands. This enables intuitive,\ntouchless control over projected media without requiring physical screens or hand-\nheld hardware.\nTable 1 presents a comparative analysis of key features among various applications.\nThe comparison includes crucial functionalities for effective management. The compar-\nison aims to highlight the unique offerings of each application and to emphasize the\ncomprehensive capabilities of the Proposed System.\nTable 1: Applications Comparison\nF eatures\nApplications\nSurfaBeam [9]\nLazyLighting [10]\nProMapper [11]\nHoloMapper [12]\nSpatialVision\nReal-Time 3D Surface Tracking ✓ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\nContinued on next page\n[PAGE 10]\nTable 1 –Continued from previous page\nF eatures\nApplications\nApp 1 [9]\nApp 2 [10]\nApp 3 [11]\nApp 4 [12]\nProposed System\nTouchless Gesture Control ✓ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\nWireless Mobile Control App ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\nSelf-Aligning Projection Loop ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\nHygienic Display Interface ✗ ✓ ✗ ✓ ✓\nDynamic Preset Switching ✓ ✗ ✗ ✓ ✓\nLive Mobile Stream Preview ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓ ✓\nInteractive Projection Mini-Games ✓ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\n8.6 Modules Description\nModule 1: Camera & Projector Hardware Interface\n•Webcam configuration (resolution, FPS, exposure)\n•Projector output and multi-monitor setup\n•Camera-projector frame synchronization\n•Latency measurement and compensation\nModule 2: Mobile Application Interface (Android Native)\n•Deliver an Android native app for remote projection management.\n•Display live camera previews, system diagnostics, and calibration controls.\n•Enable seamless switching between projection presets, scenes, and mini-games.\n•Ensure real-time, low-latency wireless communication using WebSockets.\nModule 3: Backend and API Integration (FastAPI)\n•Handle server-side logic and AI model integration.\n•Manage RESTful APIs for system configurations and preset data.\n•Use WebSockets for real-time mobile control and live previews.\n•Ensure secure local authentication and device authorization.\nModule 4: Hand Tracking & Gesture Recognition (OpenCV / MediaPipe)\n•HD webcam frame capture and preprocessing\n[PAGE 11]\n•21-point 3D hand landmark tracking (MediaPipe)\n•Gesture recognition (point, pinch, swipe, palm)\n•Gesture-to-projection coordinate mapping\nModule 5: Preset & Content Management\n•Manage preset visual layouts, interactive scenes, and game templates.\n•Handle dynamic content switching and scene transitions.\n•Parse scene configuration profiles and custom layout parameters.\n•Provide administrative tools for preset creation and content curation.\nModule 6: Projection Graphics & Mini-Games\n•Real-time scene and UI rendering\n•Gesture-driven hover, select, and drag events\n•Gesture-reactive mini-games with scoring\n•Dynamic scene loading from presets\nModule 7: Automatic Projector-Camera Calibration (Homography)\n•ArUco marker projection and detection\n•Automatic homography matrix computation\n•Closed-loop reprojection error correction\n•Auto re-calibration on surface movement\nModule 8: Usage & Performance Analytics\n•Session duration and gesture count tracking\n•FPS, latency, and CPU/GPU monitoring\n•Tracking accuracy and calibration error logs\n•Exportable CSV/JSON reports\nModule 9: Real-Time Communication\n•REST API endpoints for system control and status updates\n•Low-latency WebSocket communication for real-time data exchange\n•MJPEG video streaming for live mobile previews\n•Heartbeat and automatic reconnection for stable communication\nModule 10: Surface Warping & Distortion Correction\n•Perspective warping using homography\n•Mesh-based keystone distortion correction\n[PAGE 12]\n•Edge blending and surface masking\n•Real-time warp update on surface movement\nModule 11: Security & Network Access Control\n•Pairing code and JWT authentication\n•Role-based access (admin/viewer)\n•LAN-only access for authorized devices\n•Local camera processing with no cloud upload\nModule 12: Preset Storage & Asset Management System\n•Store and manage projection assets, game settings, and surface coordinates.\n•Quickly load visual presets and calibration data from the local database.\n•Save manual calibration changes even after restarting the system.\n•Use structured JSON data to keep system information synchronized.\n9. Modules\nTable 2 provides a breakdown of project modules and assigned students based on\ndeadlines, showcasing their roles and responsibilities.\nTable 2: Module Distribution Among Students\nDeadline Modules Student\nP1 Mid Camera & Projector Hardware Interface Zahra Tul Hussan Syed\n(23108195)\nMobile Application Interface (Android\nNative)\nM Anas Bhatti (23108243)\nBackend and API Integration (FastAPI) Syed Ali Tarikh (23108189)\nP1 Final Hand Tracking & Gesture Recognition\n(OpenCV / MediaPipe)\nZahra Tul Hussan Syed\n(23108195)\nPreset & Content Management Syed Ali Tarikh (23108189)\nProjection Graphics & Mini-Games M Anas Bhatti (23108243)\nP2 Mid Automatic Projector-Camera Calibration\n(Homography)\nM Anas Bhatti (23108243)\nUsage & Performance Analytics Zahra Tul Hussan Syed\n(23108195)\nReal-Time Communication (FastAPI /\nWebSocket)\nSyed Ali Tarikh (23108189)\nContinued on next page\n[PAGE 13]\nTable 2 –Continued from previous page\nDeadline Modules Student\nP2 Final Surface Warping & Distortion Correction Zahra Tul Hussan Syed\n(23108195)\nSecurity & Network Access Control Syed Ali Tarikh (23108189)\npreset Storage & Asset Management\nSystem\nM Anas Bhatti (23108243)\n10. Development Environment\nTools: •Python: Backend system logic, computer vision pipelines, and sys-\ntem orchestration.\n•Android Studio & Kotlin: Native Android management app for\nmobile control..\n•OpenCV: Real-time video processing, feature tracking, and homog-\nraphy matrix calculations.\n•MediaPipe: Machine learning pipeline for 21 3D hand landmark\ntracking and gesture recognition.\n•VS Code / Android Studio: Primary IDEs for coding, app building,\nand debugging.\n•Git & GitHub: Version control system and source code repository\nmanagement.\nDBMS: •Firebase Firestore / SQLite: Real-time database for storing system\nprofiles, calibration settings, and layout presets.\nPlatform: •Windows 11 / Linux (Ubuntu): Host OS environment running\nreal-time vision algorithms and projection pipelines.\n•WebSockets / REST API: Networking protocols for real-time con-\ntrol between the mobile app and Python backend.\n11. Significance\nTraditional Spatial Augmented Reality (SAR) systems often depend on fixed geome-\ntries and manual alignment, making them difficult to use in dynamic environments. This\nproject addresses these limitations by introducing a self-aligning and touchless projection\nframework. In environments such as museums, retail spaces, and interactive classrooms,\nobjects may be moved or rearranged frequently. Automated recalibration and hand-\ngesture interaction can reduce manual effort while providing a more flexible and hygienic\nuser experience.\n[PAGE 14]\nThe project combines real-time computer vision-based pose tracking, perspective\ntransformation, and MediaPipe gesture detection into a low-latency control pipeline. A\nmobile control application further allows users to manage calibration and display content\nwirelessly without requiring specialized hardware or advanced technical skills. This can\nreduce maintenance requirements and make the system easier to operate.\nBy using affordable webcams, standard projectors, and open-source software, the\nproposed framework aims to provide a scalable and cost-effective solution for spatial\ncomputing. It supports the growing use of touchless interaction and can make interactive\nprojection experiences more accessible in educational, commercial, and public indoor\nenvironments.\n12. Tools and Techniques\nThe core implementation of this project combines real-time computer vision algo-\nrithms, machine learning frameworks, dynamic rendering engines, and a dedicated mobile\nmanagement application to achieve an adaptive projection mapping system. Spatial sur-\nface tracking relies on OpenCV for feature detection, pose estimation, and homography\nmatrix calculations that warp digital graphics to match shifting 3D physical surfaces in\nreal time. Touchless user interaction is handled by Google’s MediaPipe framework, which\ndetects 21 3D hand keypoints from incoming video feeds to translate hand gestures into\ninteractive control triggers. A cross-platform mobile application built with Flutter com-\nmunicates with a central Python backend over Wi-Fi via WebSocket protocols, allowing\nusers to remotely toggle camera detection feeds, adjust projector calibration parameters,\nand manage content navigation. The complete pipeline runs on a central PC workstation\nconnected to a high-frame-rate HD webcam, a digital projector, and rigid target display\nobjects operating in a controlled indoor setup.\n•Mobile App Framework: Android Native (Kotlin/Java) for building a dedicated\nmobile management app that remotely controls camera feeds, projector calibration\nsettings, and system navigation.\n•Network and Communication Protocols: WebSockets and RESTful APIs imple-\nmented using FastAPI in Python to enable low-latency, real-time wireless commu-\nnication between the mobile app and the PC processing engine\n•Programming Languages: Python for backend system orchestration, computer vi-\nsion processing, and matrix transformations; Dart for mobile application develop-\nment.\n•Computer Vision Framework: OpenCV (opencv-python) to manage live camera\nstreams, feature detection, pose estimation, and 3×3 homography perspective\nwarping.\n[PAGE 15]\n•Gesture Recognition Pipeline: MediaPipe Hands to extract 21 3D hand landmark\ncoordinates for touchless gesture detection (swiping, tapping, selecting) without\nphysical sensors.\n•Hardware Configuration: A standard 1080p USB webcam (30–60 FPS), an HD dig-\nital projector, a host PC workstation, a mobile smartphone, and rigid 3D geometric\nobjects in a controlled indoor space.\n[PAGE 16]\nReferences\n[1] O. Bimber and R. Raskar, Spatial Augmented Reality: Merging Real and Virtual\nWorlds. A K Peters/CRC Press, 2005. Available: https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/\n~dyer/cs534/papers/SAR.pdf(Accessed: 23 September 2026).\n[2] “AI-Dentify: Deep learning for proximal caries detection on bitewing x-ray–HUNT4\nOral Health Study.” Available at: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/\ns12903-024-04120-0(Accessed: 23 September 2026).\n[3] D. Iwai and S. Hiura, “Dynamic projection mapping onto deforming objects,” IEEE\nComputer Graphics and Applications, vol. 35, no. 5, pp. 76–85, 2015. Available at: \nhttps: //doi.org/10.1109/MCG.2015.110(Accessed: 23 September 2026).\n[4] S. Zollmann et al., “FlyAR: Augmented reality supported micro aerial vehicle naviga-\ntion,” IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph., vol. 20, no. 4, pp. 560–568, 2014. Available\nat: https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2014.24(Accessed: 23 September 2026).\n[5] C. Lugaresi et al., “MediaPipe: A framework for building perception pipelines,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv: 1906.08172, 2019. Available at:https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08172\n(Accessed: 23 September 2026).\n[6] M. S. E. Grundh¨ ofer and D. Iwai, “Recent advances in projection-based augmented\nreality,” Computers and Graphics, vol. 74, pp. 1–12, 2018. Available at: https://\npmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11105974/(Accessed: 23 September 2026).\n[7] J. Park and S. Lee, “Interactive spatial augmented reality using depth sensors and\nreal-time projector calibration,” Sensors, vol. 20, no. 12, p. 3412, 2020. Available at: \nhttps: //doi.org/10.3390/s20123412(Accessed: 23 September 2026).\n[8] T. Narita et al., “Dynamic projection mapping onto a moving planar surface with\nlow latency,” IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph., vol. 23, no. 11, pp. 2410–2419,\n2017. Available at: https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2017.2744318(Accessed: 23\nSeptember 2026).\n[9] SURF ABEAMPRO. Available at: https://www.surfabeam.com/(Accessed: 23\nSeptember 2026).\n[10] Lazy Lighting. Available at: https://lazylighting.com/(Accessed: 23 September\n2026).\n[11] ProMapper. Available at: https://projectionmapping.app/(Accessed: 23\nSeptember 2026).\n[PAGE 17]\n[12] HoloMapper. Available at: https://holomapperstudio.com/(Accessed: 23\nSeptember 2026).	{"scope": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "title": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "topic": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "purpose": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "warnings": ["topic was not detected in the uploaded document.", "objectives was not detected in the uploaded document.", "methodology was not detected in the uploaded document.", "scope was not detected in the uploaded document.", "expected_outcomes was not detected in the uploaded document."], "margin_mm": 31.59, "page_size": "A4", "objectives": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "page_count": 17, "font_family": "CMR12", "methodology": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "table_count": 2, "figure_count": 1, "font_size_pt": 11.96, "line_spacing": 0.5, "top_margin_mm": 134.77, "other_sections": [{"value": "[PAGE 1]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Final Year Project Long Proposal", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Interactive Projection Mapping", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Framework Using Computer Vision", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and Spatial Tracking", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Product Name: SpatialVision", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Syed Ali Tarikh (23108189)", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Zahra Tul Hussan Syed (23108195)", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "M. Anas Bhatti (23108243)", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Supervised by: Mr. Saad Irfan Khan", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Department of Robotics & Artificial Intelligence", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Faculty of Computing and Engineering Sciences", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "SZABIST University", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Islamabad, Pakistan", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Fall 2026", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 2]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Revision History", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Compiled By Checked By Date Description V ersion", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "M Anas Bhatti Mr. Saad Irfan Khan 24-Sept-2026 Section 1 1.0", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Zahra Tul Hussan Syed Mr. Saad Irfan Khan 01-Sept-2026 Section 2 1.1", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Syed Ali Tarikh Mr. Saad Irfan Khan 05-Oct-2026 Section 3 1.2", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 3]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "1. Project Stream", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "✓ Mobile App", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "2. Introduction", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Introduction In recent years, the convergence of display technologies and spatial com-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "puting has redefined how digital media interacts with the physical world. Traditional", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "visual display systems rely heavily on flat, two-dimensional screens or headsets to present", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "augmented content, restricting user engagement within fixed rectangular boundaries [1].", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Spatial Augmented Reality (SAR), widely known as projection mapping, overcomes these", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "physical constraints by utilizing everyday physical objects as dynamic display surfaces [2].", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "By precise alignment of visual projections over complex 3D surfaces, projection mapping", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "merges digital graphics seamlessly with tangible geometry, unlocking immersive experi-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ences across digital art, retail product showcases, medical visualization, and interactive", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "education.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Despite its growing popularity, conventional projection mapping setups remain largely", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "static and non-interactive. Traditional systems require pre-calculated calibration and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "manual alignment tailored strictly to fixed projector-object positions [3]. Any slight", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "movement or shift in the physical object breaks the visual alignment, leading to noticeable", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "distortion and visual artifacts. Furthermore, most existing setups lack real-time user", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "responsiveness, treating the viewer merely as a passive observer rather than an active", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "participant. Relying on external handheld controllers or physical touch panels to interact", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "with projected content further disrupts the natural, touchless interaction model that", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "spatial displays inherently promise.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "By integrating continuous spatial tracking with real-time pose estimation and touch-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "less gesture interaction, this project advances SAR beyond static installations into adap-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tive, touchless interactive environments. Eliminating the need for manual re-calibration", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "when objects shift significantly reduces technical overhead in interactive displays. Beyond", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "its immediate application as a functional prototype, this work aligns with broader indus-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "try shifts toward ambient computing, spatial human-computer interaction, and touchless", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "display interfaces across interactive retail, smart educational tools, and immersive simu-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "lation environments.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "3. Project Description", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Traditional projection mapping systems are static, requiring manual calibration that", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "breaks whenever an object moves [3]. This project solves that problem by building an", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 4]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "interactive, touchless projection system that adapts visuals to object movement in real", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "time. The main goal is to track physical objects as they shift or rotate and dynamically", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "warp projected graphics so they stay perfectly aligned. To make interaction natural, the", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "system uses computer vision to recognize hand gestures, letting users control projected", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "content without touchscreens or handheld controllers. The final outcome is a working", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "prototype for rigid 3D objects [4] in indoor settings, providing an intuitive interface for", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "interactive retail, smart classrooms, and public exhibits.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The underlying pipeline processes video input using Python and OpenCV to detect", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "surface boundaries and track spatial orientation in 3D space [4]. Estimated pose co-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ordinates (x, y, zposition and rotation) feed directly into a rendering pipeline built in", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Unity, which warps and transforms the graphics before projecting them back onto the", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "object. In parallel, MediaPipe analyzes camera frames to detect hand landmarks and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "track gestures, translating movements into control commands like swapping projected", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "animations or selecting menu items [5]. A calibrated camera-projector setup closes the", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "feedback loop, continuously matching real-time computer vision tracking with physical", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "projection output.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "By combining low-latency tracking algorithms with touchless gesture interaction, this", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "project demonstrates how spatial computing can operate seamlessly without expensive", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "headset hardware or rigid, permanent physical mountings. The system bridges the gap", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "between digital content and physical physical geometry, reducing the setup overhead", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "usually required for interactive spatial displays. Ultimately, this approach establishes", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "a scalable baseline for future spatial augmented reality applications, paving the way", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "for adaptive interfaces in indoor advertising, immersive educational tools, and smart", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "environments.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "4. Real World Problem", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "In modern industries such as retail showcases, museum exhibitions, digital education,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and architectural visualization, spatial computing and Spatial Augmented Reality (SAR)", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "are increasingly relied upon to create engaging customer and visitor experiences. Tra-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ditional display technologies depend heavily on flat 2D screens or personal headsets like", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "VR/AR goggles. However, flat displays constrain digital media within fixed rectangular", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "borders, while headsets isolate users from one another, preventing shared social interac-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tions. Projection mapping overcomes these physical barriers by transforming everyday", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "physical objects into dynamic display surfaces, merging digital imagery directly onto", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tangible 3D geometry without requiring users to wear cumbersome hardware [6].", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Despite its growing adoption, traditional projection mapping suffers from significant", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "operational limitations that hinder its widespread deployment in real-world environments.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Conventional setups rely on pre-calculated geometric alignments tailored strictly to fixed", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 5]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "projector and object positions [3]. If a target object is moved, rotated, or accidentally", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "bumped, the projected content immediately misaligns, leading to noticeable visual dis-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tortion and breaking the immersive experience. Re-aligning the projection manually", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "requires specialized technical expertise and substantial downtime, making existing setups", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "impractical for dynamic environments like retail spaces, museum exhibits, or interactive", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "classrooms where physical layout changes occur frequently [7].", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Furthermore, most existing projection systems lack real-time user responsiveness,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "treating the viewer merely as a passive observer. When interaction is offered, it typically", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "depends on physical touch surfaces, touch sensors, or handheld controllers. In public or", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "shared environments, physical contact interfaces raise hygiene concerns, suffer from me-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "chanical wear and tear, and disrupt the natural, touchless interaction model that spatial", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "displays inherently promise. Without continuous spatial tracking and intuitive touchless", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "control, spatial augmented reality remains confined to costly, static installations, limiting", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "user engagement and imposing high technical overhead on organizations.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "5. Problem Statement", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Spatial Augmented Reality (SAR) has transformed interactive media by projecting", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "visual content directly onto physical 3D surfaces, eliminating the need for headsets or flat", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "screens [1]. However, its effectiveness in dynamic environments—such as retail displays,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "museums, and educational spaces—is severely restricted by traditional calibration meth-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ods. When target objects move, rotate, or shift positions, the projected visuals instantly", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "misalign, resulting in severe visual distortion, broken immersion, and high manual setup", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "overhead for non-technical users.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Conventional projection mapping systems lack automated real-time spatial tracking", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and touchless user interaction, causing immediate visual alignment failures whenever", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "target objects shift position and forcing reliance on physical controllers or touch panels.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "This static nature limits spatial displays to rigid, non-interactive installations that require", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "frequent technical intervention. Without continuous computer vision tracking, existing", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "setups fail to maintain real-time alignment or support natural, touchless interaction for", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "dynamic user environments [8].", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Failing to address these tracking and interaction bottlenecks restricts SAR from adopt-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ing intuitive, ambient computing models across public and commercial domains. Relying", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "on physical touch interfaces or manual recalibration increases operational maintenance,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "creates hygiene concerns in public spaces, and degrades user experience. Developing a", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "self-aligning, gesture-controlled projection system is essential to bridge the gap between", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "static projection and adaptive, touchless spatial interaction.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 6]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "6. Names of existing similar FYPs (if repeated)", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "N/A.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "7. New Functionalities (main) to be added Features", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "N/A.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8. Application Review", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Commercial projection mapping software like HeavyM, MadMapper, and Resolume", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Arena are widely used for visual setups in stage performances, digital art installations,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and commercial exhibits. HeavyM and MadMapper focus on geometric warping, grid", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "mapping, and edge blending for visual designers and event technicians. Resolume Arena,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "on the other hand, is built primarily for live video mixing and performance setups. These", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tools offer rich visual effect libraries, multi-projector support, and timeline controls that", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "allow operators to manually align digital content onto physical 3D structures with high", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "graphical quality.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Despite their rendering capabilities, current commercial tools struggle in dynamic", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "environments because they rely entirely on static, manual setup. If a physical object", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "shifts, rotates, or gets bumped, the software cannot adjust the graphics on its own. The", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "projected image immediately distorts until a technician manually recalibrates the grid.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Additionally, these platforms do not include built-in computer vision for touchless inter-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "action. Users must rely on physical controllers, keyboard shortcuts, or MIDI equipment", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "to change content, creating technical friction for everyday users in interactive retail or", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "educational spaces.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The system developed in this project solves these problems by combining real time", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "spatial tracking and camera based gesture recognition directly into the rendering pipeline.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "By using continuous camera input to track object movement and hand gestures, the", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "application automatically warps the projected graphics to match physical movement while", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "enabling touchless control.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.1 SurfaBeam [9]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "SurfaBeam Pro is a cross-platform projection mapping app that lets creators warp,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "mask, and align video onto physical surfaces straight from a mobile device or PC no bulky", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "media servers or expensive setups required.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The key characteristics of the application are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Touch-Optimized Real-Time Engine: Leverages on-device GPU processing so you", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "can warp meshes, pin corners, and control timelines directly with touch gestures", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 7]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "without lag.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Universal Interface Across Devices: Delivers the exact same layout, performance,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and workflow whether you run it on iOS, Android, macOS, or Windows", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•100% Offline and Private: Runs entirely on local hardware with zero cloud render-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ing, accounts, or background data tracking.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of the application are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•No Automated Cloud Syncing: Projects don’t sync between platforms automati-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "cally, so files have to be moved manually between devices.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Gated Free Tier: The trial mode adds a watermark, displays a launcher prompt,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and locks project saving and exporting behind the Pro license.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Bandwidth and Cable Dependency: Output stability relies heavily on a high-quality", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "display adapter (USB-C/HDMI) or a stable 5GHz Wi-Fi link to prevent frame drops", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "during live projection.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.2 Lazy Lightning [10]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Lazy Lighting (by Audity Apps LLC) is a mobile-first projection mapping application", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "for iOS and Android. It lets creators, VJs, and DIY decorators shape, warp, and align", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "video effects or personal media onto real-world objects directly from a phone or tablet.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The key characteristics of the application are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Audio and MIDI Reactive Controls: Features over 100 generated live visuals that", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "dynamically sync to tempo, microphone input, or MIDI controllers.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Comprehensive Mapping Toolkit: Includes touch-native corner warping, masks,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "curved shapes, box mapping, and drawing tools directly on mobile devices.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Standalone ”Lazy Lighting Link” Offloading: Allows users to export and transfer", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "completed shows to dedicated TV/projector receiver apps to play without keeping", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "the phone connected.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of the application are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Single-Projector Focus: Built specifically for simple single-display setups, lacking", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "multi-projector edge blending and advanced spatial calibration required for large", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "architectural mapping.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Pay walled Feature Set: Key mapping features, show export capabilities, and ad-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "vanced visual templates are locked behind a paid Pro subscription tier.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 8]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.3 Pro Mapper [11]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Pro Mapper is an iOS-exclusive mobile projection mapping solution that turns any", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "iPhone or iPad into an instant spatial visual studio. Powered by Apple’s Metal GPU", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "engine, it allows event creators, visual artists, and performers to warp meshes, trigger", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "audio-reactive effects, and drive interactive 3D portals on physical surfaces without bring-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ing a laptop.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The key characteristics of the application are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Advanced Metal GPU Performance: Powered by Apple’s Metal rendering engine", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "to deliver real-time, touch-reactive fluid visualizers and low-latency multi-surface", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "warping.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Interactive FX and Head-Tracking Portals: Includes unique creative capabilities", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "such as head-tracked 3D projection boxes, live person segmentation/glowing out-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "lines, and animated typography.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Multi-Layer Timeline Control: Features a unified global timeline with individual", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "lanes, clip layering, stroke-by-stroke drawing animations, and direct video export", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "to Photos.connected.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of the application are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Apple Ecosystem Exclusivity: Available only on iOS, iPadOS, and macOS (M-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "series), completely excluding Android and Windows operating systems.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Single-Display Output Constraints: Lacks multi-projector edge blending and ad-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "vanced spatial calibration required for large-scale architectural mapping installa-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tions.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.4 Holo Mapper [12]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "A node-based visual programming environment built for custom, highly interactive", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "installations driven by live sensors, cameras, and audio feeds.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The key characteristics of the application are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Subscription-Free Flat-Rate Pricing: Available as a one-time purchase on Windows", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and SteamOS without recurring monthly subscription fees.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Multi-Format Local Playback: Easily loads and warps video files, animated GIFs,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "image loops, and direct live capture feeds onto physical targets.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Lightweight Desktop Engine: Designed for fast deployment on standard PCs and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "handheld SteamOS devices with low CPU/GPU resource overhead.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 9]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of the application are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•No Multi-Projector Edge Blending: Built for single-display mapping setups, making", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "it unsuitable for complex multi-projector architectural displays.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Basic Toolset: Lacks advanced VJ features like audio-reactive effect generators,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "DMX/LED lighting integration, and complex timeline sequencing.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.5Product: SpatialVision (Proposed)", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "This project develops a self-aligning projection mapping system using computer vision", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "to track object movements in real time.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Automatically warps projected graphics onto shifted 3D surfaces and detects hand", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "gestures to eliminate manual recalibration while enabling touchless control.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The key characteristics of the application are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Real-time Surface and Pose Tracking uses computer vision to continuously monitor", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "3D object movement and rotation in physical space. This allows the system to", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "dynamically warp projected graphics so that they stay perfectly aligned without", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "manual recalibration.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Touchless Gesture Control captures user hand movements through camera feed-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "back to translate gestures into direct interactive commands. This enables intuitive,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "touchless control over projected media without requiring physical screens or hand-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "held hardware.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Table 1 presents a comparative analysis of key features among various applications.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The comparison includes crucial functionalities for effective management. The compar-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ison aims to highlight the unique offerings of each application and to emphasize the", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "comprehensive capabilities of the Proposed System.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Table 1: Applications Comparison", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "F eatures", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Applications", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "SurfaBeam [9]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "LazyLighting [10]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ProMapper [11]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "HoloMapper [12]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "SpatialVision", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Real-Time 3D Surface Tracking ✓ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Continued on next page", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 10]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Table 1 –Continued from previous page", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "F eatures", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Applications", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "App 1 [9]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "App 2 [10]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "App 3 [11]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "App 4 [12]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}], "page_numbering": false, "required_fields": ["title", "purpose"], "right_margin_mm": 58.21, "bottom_margin_mm": 157.99, "detected_headings": [{"name": "1. Project Stream", "page": 3}, {"name": "2. Introduction", "page": 3}, {"name": "3. Project Description", "page": 3}, {"name": "4. Real World Problem", "page": 4}, {"name": "5. Problem Statement", "page": 5}, {"name": "6. Names of existing similar FYPs (if repeated)", "page": 6}, {"name": "7. New Functionalities (main) to be added Features", "page": 6}, {"name": "8. Application Review", "page": 6}, {"name": "8.1 SurfaBeam [9]", "page": 6}, {"name": "8.2 Lazy Lightning [10]", "page": 7}, {"name": "8.3 Pro Mapper [11]", "page": 8}, {"name": "8.4 Holo Mapper [12]", "page": 8}, {"name": "8.6 Modules Description", "page": 10}, {"name": "9. Modules", "page": 12}, {"name": "10. Development Environment", "page": 13}, {"name": "11. Significance", "page": 13}, {"name": "12. Tools and Techniques", "page": 14}, {"name": "2017. Available at: https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2017.2744318(Accessed: 23", "page": 16}], "expected_outcomes": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "proposed_solution": {"value": "Touchless Gesture Control ✓ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓ Wireless Mobile Control App ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ Self-Aligning Projection Loop ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓ Hygienic Display Interface ✗ ✓ ✗ ✓ ✓ Dynamic Preset Switching ✓ ✗ ✗ ✓ ✓ Live Mobile Stream Preview ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓ ✓ Interactive Projection Mini-Games ✓ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓ 8.6 Modules Description Module 1: Camera & Projector Hardware Interface •Webcam configuration (resolution, FPS, exposure) •Projector output and multi-monitor setup •Camera-projector frame synchronization •Latency measurement and compensation Module 2: Mobile Application Interface (Android Native) •Deliver an Android native app for remote projection management. •Display live camera previews, system diagnostics, and calibration controls. •Enable seamless switching between projection presets, scenes, and mini-games. •Ensure real-time, low-latency wireless communication using WebSockets. Module 3: Backend and API Integration (FastAPI) •Handle server-side logic and AI model integration. •Manage RESTful APIs for system configurations and preset data. •Use WebSockets for real-time mobile control and live previews. •Ensure secure local authentication and device authorization. Module 4: Hand Tracking & Gesture Recognition (OpenCV / MediaPipe) •HD webcam frame capture and preprocessing [PAGE 11] •21-point 3D hand landmark tracking (MediaPipe) •Gesture recognition (point, pinch, swipe, palm) •Gesture-to-projection coordinate mapping Module 5: Preset & Content Management •Manage preset visual layouts, interactive scenes, and game templates. •Handle dynamic content switching and scene transitions. •Parse scene configuration profiles and custom layout parameters. •Provide administrative tools for preset creation and content curation. Module 6: Projection Graphics & Mini-Games •Real-time scene and UI rendering •Gesture-driven hover, select, and drag events •Gesture-reactive mini-games with scoring •Dynamic scene loading from presets Module 7: Automatic Projector-Camera Calibration (Homography) •ArUco marker projection and detection •Automatic homography matrix computation •Closed-loop reprojection error correction •Auto re-calibration on surface movement Module 8: Usage & Performance Analytics •Session duration and gesture count tracking •FPS, latency, and CPU/GPU monitoring •Tracking accuracy and calibration error logs •Exportable CSV/JSON reports Module 9: Real-Time Communication •REST API endpoints for system control and status updates •Low-latency WebSocket communication for real-time data exchange •MJPEG video streaming for live mobile previews •Heartbeat and automatic reconnection for stable communication Module 10: Surface Warping & Distortion Correction •Perspective warping using homography •Mesh-based keystone distortion correction [PAGE 12] •Edge blending and surface masking •Real-time warp update on surface movement Module 11: Security & Network Access Control •Pairing code and JWT authentication •Role-based access (admin/viewer) •LAN-only access for authorized devices •Local camera processing with no cloud upload Module 12: Preset Storage & Asset Management System •Store and manage projection assets, game settings, and surface coordinates. •Quickly load visual presets and calibration data from the local database. •Save manual calibration changes even after restarting the system. •Use structured JSON data to keep system information synchronized. 9. Modules Table 2 provides a breakdown of project modules and assigned students based on deadlines, showcasing their roles and responsibilities. Table 2: Module Distribution Among Students Deadline Modules Student P1 Mid Camera & Projector Hardware Interface Zahra Tul Hussan Syed (23108195) Mobile Application Interface (Android Native) M Anas Bhatti (23108243) Backend and API Integration (FastAPI) Syed Ali Tarikh (23108189) P1 Final Hand Tracking & Gesture Recognition (OpenCV / MediaPipe) Zahra Tul Hussan Syed (23108195) Preset & Content Management Syed Ali Tarikh (23108189) Projection Graphics & Mini-Games M Anas Bhatti (23108243) P2 Mid Automatic Projector-Camera Calibration (Homography) M Anas Bhatti (23108243) Usage & Performance Analytics Zahra Tul Hussan Syed (23108195) Real-Time Communication (FastAPI / WebSocket) Syed Ali Tarikh (23108189) Continued on next page [PAGE 13] Table 2 –Continued from previous page Deadline Modules Student P2 Final Surface Warping & Distortion Correction Zahra Tul Hussan Syed (23108195) Security & Network Access Control Syed Ali Tarikh (23108189) preset Storage & Asset Management System M Anas Bhatti (23108243) 10. Development Environment Tools: •Python: Backend system logic, computer vision pipelines, and sys- tem orchestration. •Android Studio & Kotlin: Native Android management app for mobile control.. •OpenCV: Real-time video processing, feature tracking, and homog- raphy matrix calculations. •MediaPipe: Machine learning pipeline for 21 3D hand landmark tracking and gesture recognition. •VS Code / Android Studio: Primary IDEs for coding, app building, and debugging. •Git & GitHub: Version control system and source code repository management. DBMS: •Firebase Firestore / SQLite: Real-time database for storing system profiles, calibration settings, and layout presets. Platform: •Windows 11 / Linux (Ubuntu): Host OS environment running real-time vision algorithms and projection pipelines. •WebSockets / REST API: Networking protocols for real-time con- trol between the mobile app and Python backend. 11. Significance Traditional Spatial Augmented Reality (SAR) systems often depend on fixed geome- tries and manual alignment, making them difficult to use in dynamic environments. This project addresses these limitations by introducing a self-aligning and touchless projection framework. In environments such as museums, retail spaces, and interactive classrooms, objects may be moved or rearranged frequently. Automated recalibration and hand- gesture interaction can reduce manual effort while providing a more flexible and hygienic user experience. [PAGE 14] The project combines real-time computer vision-based pose tracking, perspective transformation, and MediaPipe gesture detection into a low-latency control pipeline. A mobile control application further allows users to manage calibration and display content wirelessly without requiring specialized hardware or advanced technical skills. This can reduce maintenance requirements and make the system easier to operate. By using affordable webcams, standard projectors, and open-source software, the proposed framework aims to provide a scalable and cost-effective solution for spatial computing. It supports the growing use of touchless interaction and can make interactive projection experiences more accessible in educational, commercial, and public indoor environments. 12. Tools and Techniques The core implementation of this project combines real-time computer vision algo- rithms, machine learning frameworks, dynamic rendering engines, and a dedicated mobile management application to achieve an adaptive projection mapping system. Spatial sur- face tracking relies on OpenCV for feature detection, pose estimation, and homography matrix calculations that warp digital graphics to match shifting 3D physical surfaces in real time. Touchless user interaction is handled by Google’s MediaPipe framework, which detects 21 3D hand keypoints from incoming video feeds to translate hand gestures into interactive control triggers. A cross-platform mobile application built with Flutter com- municates with a central Python backend over Wi-Fi via WebSocket protocols, allowing users to remotely toggle camera detection feeds, adjust projector calibration parameters, and manage content navigation. The complete pipeline runs on a central PC workstation connected to a high-frame-rate HD webcam, a digital projector, and rigid target display objects operating in a controlled indoor setup. •Mobile App Framework: Android Native (Kotlin/Java) for building a dedicated mobile management app that remotely controls camera feeds, projector calibration settings, and system navigation. •Network and Communication Protocols: WebSockets and RESTful APIs imple- mented using FastAPI in Python to enable low-latency, real-time wireless commu- nication between the mobile app and the PC processing engine •Programming Languages: Python for backend system orchestration, computer vi- sion processing, and matrix transformations; Dart for mobile application develop- ment. •Computer Vision Framework: OpenCV (opencv-python) to manage live camera streams, feature detection, pose estimation, and 3×3 homography perspective warping. [PAGE 15] •Gesture Recognition Pipeline: MediaPipe Hands to extract 21 3D hand landmark coordinates for touchless gesture detection (swiping, tapping, selecting) without physical sensors. •Hardware Configuration: A standard 1080p USB webcam (30–60 FPS), an HD dig- ital projector, a host PC workstation, a mobile smartphone, and rigid 3D geometric objects in a controlled indoor space. [PAGE 16] References [1] O. Bimber and R. Raskar, Spatial Augmented Reality: Merging Real and Virtual Worlds. A K Peters/CRC Press, 2005. Available: https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/ ~dyer/cs534/papers/SAR.pdf(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [2] “AI-Dentify: Deep learning for proximal caries detection on bitewing x-ray–HUNT4 Oral Health Study.” Available at: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/ s12903-024-04120-0(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [3] D. Iwai and S. Hiura, “Dynamic projection mapping onto deforming objects,” IEEE Computer Graphics and Applications, vol. 35, no. 5, pp. 76–85, 2015. Available at: https: //doi.org/10.1109/MCG.2015.110(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [4] S. Zollmann et al., “FlyAR: Augmented reality supported micro aerial vehicle naviga- tion,” IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph., vol. 20, no. 4, pp. 560–568, 2014. Available at: https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2014.24(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [5] C. Lugaresi et al., “MediaPipe: A framework for building perception pipelines,” arXiv preprint arXiv: 1906.08172, 2019. Available at:https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08172 (Accessed: 23 September 2026). [6] M. S. E. Grundh¨ ofer and D. Iwai, “Recent advances in projection-based augmented reality,” Computers and Graphics, vol. 74, pp. 1–12, 2018. Available at: https:// pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11105974/(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [7] J. Park and S. Lee, “Interactive spatial augmented reality using depth sensors and real-time projector calibration,” Sensors, vol. 20, no. 12, p. 3412, 2020. Available at: https: //doi.org/10.3390/s20123412(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [8] T. Narita et al., “Dynamic projection mapping onto a moving planar surface with low latency,” IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph., vol. 23, no. 11, pp. 2410–2419, 2017. Available at: https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2017.2744318(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [9] SURF ABEAMPRO. Available at: https://www.surfabeam.com/(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [10] Lazy Lighting. Available at: https://lazylighting.com/(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [11] ProMapper. Available at: https://projectionmapping.app/(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [PAGE 17] [12] HoloMapper. Available at: https://holomapperstudio.com/(Accessed: 23 September 2026).", "source": "page 1", "display": "Touchless Gesture Control ✓ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓ Wireless Mobile Control App ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ Self-Aligning Projecti