cation Protocols: WebSockets and RESTful APIs imple- mented using FastAPI in Python to enable low-latency, real-time wireless commu- nication between the mobile app and the PC processing engine •Programming Languages: Python for backend system orchestration, computer vi- sion processing, and matrix transformations; Dart for mobile application develop- ment. •Computer Vision Framework: OpenCV (opencv-python) to manage live camera streams, feature detection, pose estimation, and 3×3 homography perspective warping. [PAGE 15] •Gesture Recognition Pipeline: MediaPipe Hands to extract 21 3D hand landmark coordinates for touchless gesture detection (swiping, tapping, selecting) without physical sensors. •Hardware Configuration: A standard 1080p USB webcam (30–60 FPS), an HD dig- ital projector, a host PC workstation, a mobile smartphone, and rigid 3D geometric objects in a controlled indoor space. [PAGE 16] References [1] O. Bimber and R. Raskar, Spatial Augmented Reality: Merging Real and Virtual Worlds. A K Peters/CRC Press, 2005. Available: https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/ ~dyer/cs534/papers/SAR.pdf(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [2] “AI-Dentify: Deep learning for proximal caries detection on bitewing x-ray–HUNT4 Oral Health Study.” Available at: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/ s12903-024-04120-0(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [3] D. Iwai and S. Hiura, “Dynamic projection mapping onto deforming objects,” IEEE Computer Graphics and Applications, vol. 35, no. 5, pp. 76–85, 2015. Available at: https: //doi.org/10.1109/MCG.2015.110(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [4] S. Zollmann et al., “FlyAR: Augmented reality supported micro aerial vehicle naviga- tion,” IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph., vol. 20, no. 4, pp. 560–568, 2014. Available at: https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2014.24(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [5] C. Lugaresi et al., “MediaPipe: A framework for building perception pipelines,” arXiv preprint arXiv: 1906.08172, 2019. Available at:https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08172 (Accessed: 23 September 2026). [6] M. S. E. Grundh¨ ofer and D. Iwai, “Recent advances in projection-based augmented reality,” Computers and Graphics, vol. 74, pp. 1–12, 2018. Available at: https:// pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11105974/(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [7] J. Park and S. Lee, “Interactive spatial augmented reality using depth sensors and real-time projector calibration,” Sensors, vol. 20, no. 12, p. 3412, 2020. Available at: https: //doi.org/10.3390/s20123412(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [8] T. Narita et al., “Dynamic projection mapping onto a moving planar surface with low latency,” IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph., vol. 23, no. 11, pp. 2410–2419, 2017. Available at: https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2017.2744318(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [9] SURF ABEAMPRO. Available at: https://www.surfabeam.com/(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [10] Lazy Lighting. Available at: https://lazylighting.com/(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [11] ProMapper. Available at: https://projectionmapping.app/(Accessed: 23 September 2026). [PAGE 17] [12] HoloMapper. Available at: https://holomapperstudio.com/(Accessed: 23 September 2026).", "source_page": 1}, "missing_required_fields": ["title", "purpose"]}	1256	\N	2026-11-05 12:31:02.013241+00	2026-11-05 12:31:02.013241+00	\N
4	7a9d6c75-886a-4c75-af08-0dab3124d4f5	34847	LONG	long proposal	1	SUBMITTED	COMPLETED	long proposal.pdf	application/pdf	244243	6f4f9b177a71ad2bf7d947c9b5272c262aa4f1d52c7d177bef80a0f89437da3a	17	module-3\\submissions\\7a9d6c75-886a-4c75-af08-0dab3124d4f5\\v1-long_proposal.pdf	[PAGE 1]\nFinal Year Project Long Proposal\nFYPHub: An Agentic AI-Driven\nPlatform for Intelligent Final Year Project\nManagement\nMuhammad Maaz 23108179\nZain Ali Kiani 23108197\nDanish Faraz 23108160\nSupervised by: Sir Hamza Imran\nDepartment of Robotics & Artificial Intelligence\nFaculty of Computing and Engineering Sciences\nSZABIST University\nIslamabad, Pakistan\nFall 2026\n[PAGE 2]\nRevision History\nCompiled By Checked By Date Description V ersion\nMuhammad\nMaaz\nSir Hamza Imran 07-Oct-2026 Long proposal structure\nand content\n1.0\nZain Ali Kiani Sir Hamza Imran 07-Oct-2026 Module and feature\nreview\n1.1\nDanish Faraz Sir Hamza Imran 07-Oct-2026 Workflow and\ntechnology review\n1.2\nProject Team Sir Hamza Imran 07-Oct-2026 Final 12-module draft 1.3\n[PAGE 3]\n1. Project Stream\n✓ Web-Based FYP Desktop Application\nMobile Application Game\nHardware-Based\n2. Introduction\nFinal Year Project (FYP) management is a multi-stage academic process involving\nproposal processing, project allocation, collaboration, project completion, evaluation,\nand administrative coordination. Prior FYP-management research has shown that these\nactivities benefit from centralized digital workflows and role-specific collaboration tools\nrather than disconnected manual processes [1, 2]. In many university environments,\nforms, spreadsheets, emails, messaging applications, and loosely organized digital records\ncan make proposal tracking, supervisor coordination, evaluation, and reuse of previous\nproject knowledge difficult.\nFYPHubis proposed as a centralized, role-aware, AI-assisted platform for managing\nthe complete FYP lifecycle. The platform is designed for five major roles: Student, Super-\nvisor, FYP Coordinator, Panel Member, and Administrator. It combines deterministic\nacademic rules with Large Language Models (LLMs), semantic embeddings, Retrieval-\nAugmented Generation (RAG), GraphRAG, machine-learning based risk analysis, opti-\nmization techniques, and agentic AI orchestration. RAG provides language models with\nretrieved external evidence for knowledge-intensive generation, while GraphRAG extends\nretrieval with graph-structured relationships for broader and multi-hop reasoning over\ndocument collections [3, 4]. AI is used for assistance, analysis, retrieval, explanation, and\nprediction, while final academic authority remains with authorized university personnel.\nThe FYP is organized into twelve clearly separated modules. Each module owns a\ndistinct part of the FYP lifecycle so that proposal management, quality compliance, orig-\ninality checking, supervision, scheduling, evaluation, progress monitoring, institutional\nknowledge retrieval, and final archiving do not overlap unnecessarily.\n3. Project Description\nFYPHub is a responsive web-based platform that digitizes and intelligently connects\nthe complete Final Year Project process. Students can form groups, find compatible\nteammates, create and submit proposal versions, run AI-assisted compliance checks, com-\npare proposals against previous FYPs, and request suitable supervisors. Supervisors can\nreview proposals using structured comments and revision requests, while the Coordina-\ntor manages academic routing and panel readiness. The system also schedules defenses,\nsupports structured panel evaluation, tracks project progress and supervision evidence,\narchives completed FYPs, and makes institutional project knowledge searchable through\nRAG and GraphRAG.\nThe platform follows a shared-module, role-based-access architecture. A feature is not\nduplicated into separate modules merely because different roles can view it. For example,\nthe proposal compliance engine remains one module, but students, supervisors, coordina-\ntors, and panel members access different views according to their permissions. Similarly,\nthe final archive creates and preserves institutional knowledge, while the knowledge-search\nmodule retrieves and reasons over that archived information.\nThe system is expected to provide a single source of truth for proposal versions,\n[PAGE 4]\nsupervisor assignments, review comments, coordinator actions, panel evaluations, project\nprogress, and final project outcomes. This improves traceability and supports data-driven\nacademic decision making across the entire FYP lifecycle.\n4. Real World Problem\nUniversities generate a large amount of valuable FYP knowledge every semester, but\nthat knowledge is often difficult to search, compare, and reuse. Students may not know\nwhether an idea has already been implemented, what limitations previous projects had,\nwhich supervisor is most suitable, or which university formatting and proposal require-\nments are still unmet. Group formation can also be difficult when students do not have\nsuitable teammates, and duplicate membership can occur if group rules are not centrally\nenforced.\nFYP administration also involves several academic actors. Students submit propos-\nals, supervisors review them, coordinators control academic routing, panels evaluate the\nwork, and administrators configure academic rules and deadlines. When these activities\nare distributed across disconnected tools, important context is lost. Revision comments\ncan become scattered, supervisor capacity may be unclear, panel scheduling can create\nconflicts, progress evidence may remain informal, and completed projects can become\nstatic files rather than reusable institutional knowledge.\nThe real-world challenge is therefore broader than simple proposal submission. The\nuniversity needs an integrated system that manages the workflow, applies deterministic\nacademic rules, provides AI-assisted analysis where appropriate, preserves an auditable\nhistory, and converts historical FYP records into an active knowledge base for future\nstudents and faculty.\n5. Problem Statement\nCurrent FYP management practices may suffer from fragmented team formation,\nmanual proposal submission, inconsistent formatting, weak originality checks, limited\nvisibility of supervisor expertise and capacity, scattered review communication, man-\nual defense scheduling, disconnected panel feedback, limited progress evidence, and\npoor reuse of completed-project knowledge. Existing generic learning-management,\nplagiarism-detection, and project-management tools can support individual parts of this\nprocess, but they do not provide a unified FYP-specific platform that connects academic\nworkflow, proposal intelligence, supervisor allocation, panel evaluation, project progress,\nand institutional knowledge.\nThe proposed project aims to develop theFYPHubplatform, which manages the\ncomplete FYP lifecycle through twelve integrated but non-overlapping modules. The\nsystem will combine role-based workflow control with AI-assisted proposal compliance,\nsemantic originality analysis, supervisor matching, revision verification, scheduling opti-\nmization, progress and risk intelligence, RAG-based institutional knowledge search, and\nfinal project handover support.\n6. Names of Existing Similar FYPs (if repeated)\nNo exact repeated FYP has been confirmed in the current project records at proposal\nstage. However, the problem space overlaps partially with learning-management systems,\nplagiarism/similarity platforms, project-management tools, and institutional repositories.\nThe proposed FYPHub differs by integrating the complete FYP lifecycle and by using\narchived institutional FYP data as an active source for similarity analysis, knowledge\nretrieval, extension guidance, and analytics.\n[PAGE 5]\nThe proposed system will also contain its ownProposal Similarity & Originality\nCheckmodule. Once historical FYP records are available in the repository, this module\nwill compare a new proposal against previous projects and provide evidence of semantic,\nfunctional, and methodological overlap rather than relying only on title similarity.\n7. New Functionalities (main) to be added Features\nFYPHub introduces several capabilities that are not normally available together in a\nsingle FYP management platform: \n•AI-assisted teammate discovery using compatibility questions, skills, availability,\nwork style, and role preferences, with duplicate-group membership prevention.\n•University-template-aware proposal compliance checking for structure, headings,\nmargins, spacing, alignment, required sections, and academic consistency.\n•Semantic and functional comparison of new proposals against archived FYPs, in-\ncluding contribution boundaries and extension gaps.\n•Expertise, workload, preference, and capacity-based supervisor recommendation\nand allocation with slot enforcement.\n•Structured supervisor comments, revision requests, two-way discussion, and AI-\nassisted verification of whether requested revisions were resolved.\n•Coordinator-controlled academic routing with delay and workflow-conformance\nmonitoring.\n•Conflict-aware defense scheduling and panel assignment based on availability,\nroom/time constraints, and academic conflicts.\n•Panel scoring and feedback with AI-generated viva questions, evaluator disagree-\nment detection, and feedback-resolution tracking.\n•Supervision-meeting evidence, milestone tracking, project-health classification,\ndelay-risk analysis, and recovery recommendations.\n•Final FYP archiving with implemented-scope extraction, limitations, future work,\nreusable assets, and intelligent handover packages.\n•RAG/GraphRAG-based institutional FYP search with evidence and multi-hop rea-\nsoning over archived projects.\n•Institutional analytics for domain trends, workload, turnaround time, completion\npatterns, and management insights.\n8. Application Review\nThis section reviews six existing platforms that address selected parts of the FYP\nlifecycle. Each application is summarized through two key characteristics and two limita-\ntions, followed by a feature-by-application comparison table in the same tabular pattern\nused throughout this proposal. The purpose is to show which capabilities already exist\nin established systems and which FYP-specific gaps remain.\n[PAGE 6]\n8.1 Moodle Assignment Activity [5]\nMoodle provides structured assignment submission, group submissions, deadlines,\ngrading, feedback, and rubric-based assessment within a learning-management environ-\nment.\n•Key characteristic 1: Students can submit files or online text individually or in\ngroups, with configurable availability and due dates.\n•Key characteristic 2: Instructors can grade submissions, provide feedback, and\nuse rubrics or custom grading scales.\nThe limitations of Moodle for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.\n•Limitation 1: It does not provide FYP-specific supervisor expertise/capacity\nmatching, allocation optimization, or defense-panel scheduling.\n•Limitation 2: It does not provide institutional FYP semantic originality analysis,\nGraphRAG reasoning, or intelligent handover/continuation support.\n8.2 Turnitin Similarity Report [6]\nTurnitin compares submitted text against large source collections and produces a Sim-\nilarity Report that highlights matching text, source information, and an overall similarity\npercentage.\n•Key characteristic 1: Text matching against internet sources, publications, and\npreviously submitted work with source-level evidence.\n•Key characteristic 2: Similarity reports support academic review by grouping\nand presenting matched passages for human interpretation.\nThe limitations of Turnitin for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.\n•Limitation 1: It primarily measures textual similarity and does not evaluate FYP\nproblem, objectives, modules/features, methodology, implemented scope, and con-\ntribution boundaries as one semantic project comparison.\n•Limitation 2: It does not manage FYP team formation, supervisor allocation,\ncoordinator routing, defense scheduling, project-progress intelligence, or final han-\ndover.\n8.3 OpenProject [7]\nOpenProject is a general project-management and collaboration platform supporting\nplanning, tasks, milestones, workloads, timelines, and project progress.\n•Key characteristic 1: Work packages, tasks, milestones, timelines, and responsi-\nbilities can be planned and tracked across a project lifecycle.\n•Key characteristic 2: Team collaboration, workload planning, project dash-\nboards, and status tracking support coordinated project delivery.\nThe limitations of OpenProject for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.\n•Limitation 1: It does not provide university-template proposal compliance, histor-\nical FYP originality intelligence, or formal supervisor revision-compliance checking.\n•Limitation 2: It does not provide FYP-specific supervisor allocation, panel evalu-\nation, institutional FYP archive reasoning, or GraphRAG-based knowledge search.\n[PAGE 7]\n8.4 Microsoft Teams for Education [8]\nMicrosoft Teams for Education combines class communication, channels, assignments,\ndue dates, resources, feedback, and rubric-based grading in one collaborative environ-\nment.\n•Key characteristic 1: Channels and class-team communication support collabo-\nration among students and educators.\n•Key characteristic 2: Assignments support due dates, file submission, resubmis-\nsion, resources, grades, and rubrics.\nThe limitations of Microsoft Teams for Education for the target FYP lifecycle are listed\nbelow.\n•Limitation 1: It does not provide FYP-specific teammate compatibility matching,\nsupervisor expertise/capacity allocation, or historical FYP originality analysis.\n•Limitation 2: It does not manage coordinator academic routing, defense-panel\noptimization, project handover intelligence, or RAG/GraphRAG reasoning over\nFYP archives.\n8.5 GitHub Projects [9]\nGitHub Projects supports software-team planning and tracking through issues, pull\nrequests, project boards, tables, roadmaps, custom fields, and code-linked work items.\n•Key characteristic 1: Issues and Projects can organize tasks, ideas, bugs, mile-\nstones, and development work with configurable views and metadata.\n•Key characteristic 2: Work tracking is closely linked with repositories, pull re-\nquests, and software-development activity.\nThe limitations of GitHub Projects for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.\n•Limitation 1: It is development-work oriented and does not provide university\nproposal formatting/compliance, supervisor matching, or formal academic routing.\n•Limitation 2: It does not provide defense scheduling, panel rubric evaluation,\nsemantic comparison with archived FYPs, or institutional FYP handover workflows.\n8.6 DSpace Institutional Repository [10]\nDSpace is an institutional repository platform for managing, preserving, controlling\naccess to, and searching digital content and metadata.\n•Key characteristic 1: It preserves multiple digital formats and supports\nmetadata/full-text search for institutional collections.\n•Key characteristic 2: Granular access control and repository organization sup-\nport long-term institutional knowledge preservation.\nThe limitations of DSpace for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.\n•Limitation 1: It is primarily a repository and does not manage active FYP team\nformation, proposal review, supervisor allocation, progress, or defense workflows.\n•Limitation 2: Standard repository search does not provide FYP-specific seman-\ntic originality analysis, revision verification, or RAG/GraphRAG-based multi-hop\nacademic reasoning by itself.\n[PAGE 8]\n8.7 FYPHub (Our FYP)\nFYPHub combines the complete university FYP lifecycle in one role-aware platform\ninstead of treating submission, similarity checking, supervision, evaluation, progress, and\ninstitutional knowledge as disconnected activities.\n•Key characteristic 1: Integrated 12-module workflow covering team formation,\nproposal compliance/originality, supervisor allocation/revision, coordinator control,\ndefense/panel processes, project progress, final archive, and handover.\n•Key characteristic 2: AI-assisted institutional intelligence using LLMs, embed-\ndings, RAG/GraphRAG, optimization, and evidence-based analysis while retaining\nhuman academic authority.\nTable 1: Applications Comparison\nF eature Moodle[5] T urnitin[6] OpenProject[7] MST eams[8] GitHub[9] DSpace[10] FYPHub\nAssignment/document\nsubmission and deadlines\n✓ ✓ ✗ ✓ ✗ ✗ ✓\nTeam collaboration and work\ntracking\n✗ ✗ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✗ ✓\nSimilarity/originality\nevidence\n✗ ✓ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\nRubric-based\nevaluation/feedback\n✓ ✓ ✗ ✓ ✗ ✗ ✓\nInstitutional\nrepository/archive\npreservation\n✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓ ✓\nUniversity proposal\nformat/compliance analysis\n✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\nSupervisor expertise/capacity\nallocation\n✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\nHistorical FYP\nsemantic/functional\ncomparison\n✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\nDefense scheduling and panel\nassignment\n✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\nRAG/GraphRAG reasoning\nover archived FYPs\n✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\nFinal FYP\nhandover/continuation\nintelligence\n✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✓\n9. Scope\nThe scope of FYPHub covers the complete university FYP lifecycle through twelve\nintegrated modules. The system is software-based and uses role-aware workflows, de-\nterministic academic rules, and AI-assisted analysis while preserving human academic\nauthority.\n9.1 Included Aspects\n•Role-based web access for Student, Supervisor, FYP Coordinator, Panel Member,\nand Administrator users.\n[PAGE 9]\n•Student team formation, proposal submission/versioning, university-format com-\npliance, and proposal originality analysis.\n•Supervisor matching/allocation, supervisor-led proposal review/revision, coordina-\ntor routing, defense scheduling, and panel evaluation.\n•Supervision evidence, milestone/progress monitoring, project-risk assistance, and\nrecovery recommendations.\n•Final FYP archiving, handover/continuation knowledge, RAG/GraphRAG search,\nand institutional analytics.\n9.2 Excluded Aspects\n•Automatic replacement of supervisor, coordinator, panel, or administrator academic\ndecisions by AI; authorized personnel retain final authority.\n•University processes outside the FYP lifecycle such as general admissions, fee pro-\ncessing, and non-FYP academic administration.\n•Development of proprietary hardware or a native mobile application; the proposed\nsystem is a web-based software platform.\n•Use of confidential institutional or student data outside approved access permissions\nand role-based policies.\n9.3 Modules Description\nModule 1: Admin Dashboard & Academic Management\nDescription: This module provides the administrative foundation of the platform.\nIt allows authorized administrators to manage users, roles, academic cycles, proposal\ntemplates, supervisor capacity, evaluation rubrics, deadlines, reminders, escalation rules,\nand audit history.\n•User and role management for Students, Supervisors, Coordinators, Panel Members,\nand Administrators.\n•Semester and FYP-cycle configuration including group-size and workflow policies.\n•Official short-proposal and long-proposal template management with versioned aca-\ndemic rules.\n•Supervisor capacity, panel rubric, scoring criteria, and academic-deadline configu-\nration.\n•Deadline reminder/escalation rules and immutable audit history of administrative\nchanges.\nPrimary Output: Academic governance configuration and deadline action rules.\nModule 2: Student Team & Collaboration Hub\nDescription: This module manages student group formation and project collabo-\nration. It supports AI-assisted teammate discovery for students who do not yet have a\ncomplete group and enforces the university rule that a student cannot belong to more\nthan one active FYP group in the same cycle.\n[PAGE 10]\n•FYP group creation, leader/member rules, group-size enforcement, and verified\nmembership.\n•Duplicate-group membership prevention using Student ID/Registration Number\nand current FYP cycle.\n•AI teammate compatibility matching using 8–10 questions covering skills, domain,\navailability, working style, preferred role, and commitment.\n•Join requests, invitations, accept/reject workflow, student-to-group matching, and\ntemporary match chat.\n•Seven collaboration channels (General, Proposal, Research, Supervisor Discussion,\nTasks & Progress, Files & Resources, Announcements & Deadlines) plus a group\nAI chatbot.\nPrimary Output: Verified and compatible FYP team with a shared collaboration\nworkspace.\nModule 3: Student Proposal & AI Compliance Check\nDescription: This module owns the official proposal record and validates a student’s\nshort or long proposal against the university template and academic-quality rules. The\nsame analysis can be viewed by authorized supervisors, coordinators, and panel members\naccording to role permissions.\n•Short/long proposal creation, upload, draft, submit, resubmit, and lifecycle-status\nmanagement.\n•Version history with AI-generated change summaries between proposal versions.\n•University-format checking for font, font size, margins, alignment, line spacing,\nheading styles, page numbering, tables, and figures.\n•Structural validation for required sections, section order, headings, references, ap-\npendices, and captions.\n•Academic consistency analysis across problem statement, objectives, methodology,\nproposed solution, scope, and expected outcomes.\nPrimary Output: Official proposal version and Proposal Compliance & Readiness Re-\nport.\nModule 4: Proposal Similarity & Originality Check\nDescription: This module compares a new proposal with archived FYPs to deter-\nmine how much of the idea, problem, implementation scope, and methodology overlaps\nwith previous work and where a genuine contribution can begin.\n•Semantic embedding search against historical FYP records.\n•Multi-level comparison of title, problem statement, objectives, modules/features,\nand methodology.\n•Evidence showing the most related previous projects and their implemented scope.\n[PAGE 11]\n•Contribution-boundary analysis separating inherited/overlapping work from gen-\nuinely new work.\n•Gap and extension analysis based on previous limitations, missing features, and\nunfinished scope.\nPrimary Output: Originality, duplication, and contribution-boundary report.\nModule 5: Supervisor Selection & Allocation\nDescription: This module identifies suitable supervisors and manages the complete\nassignment process using expertise relevance, research interests, current workload, capac-\nity, and academic constraints.\n•AI-based supervisor expertise matching using proposal content, research interests,\nand previous supervision history.\n•Ranked supervisor recommendations with explainable match reasons and expertise\ngaps.\n•Supervisor capacity and slot management showing total, filled, and available slots.\n•Student request, supervisor accept/reject, duplicate-request protection, and final\nassignment workflow.\n•Fair allocation and conflict checking using workload, preferences, capacity, and\nacademic constraints.\nPrimary Output: Explainable supervisor recommendation and valid final assignment.\nModule 6: Supervisor Proposal Review Dashboard\nDescription: After assignment, this module manages the formal supervisor-led pro-\nposal review process. Comments and requested changes remain linked to proposal versions\nso that the academic revision history is fully traceable.\n•Paragraph-level, section-level, module-level, and full-proposal comments.\n•Editing suggestions and formal revision requests from the assigned supervisor.\n•Two-way student-supervisor discussion linked to the relevant proposal/revision\nitem.\n•Semantic comparison between old and revised proposal versions.\n•AI revision-compliance checking that classifies requested changes as Resolved, Par-\ntially Resolved, or Unresolved while leaving final authority with the supervisor.\nPrimary Output: Revision Resolution Report and approved/revised proposal history.\nModule 7: Coordinator Proposal Control\nDescription: This module gives the FYP Coordinator controlled academic routing\nover supervisor-approved proposals and provides visibility into delayed, incomplete, or\nworkflow-inconsistent cases.\n•Dedicated queue of supervisor-approved proposals ready for coordinator action.\n[PAGE 12]\n•Hold, return-to-student, return-to-supervisor, and forward-to-panel actions.\n•Version-aware, immutable history of coordinator decisions and reasons.\n•Workflow bottleneck and abnormal-delay detection across proposal stages.\n•Priority and conformance monitoring for urgent cases, skipped stages, unresolved\nrequirements, and deadline risk.\nPrimary Output: Coordinator decision record and panel-ready proposal state.\nModule 8: Panel Scheduling & Defense\nDescription: This module manages defense logistics after a proposal/project be-\ncomes eligible for panel evaluation. It allocates panel members, time slots, and rooms\nwhile preventing scheduling and academic conflicts.\n•Panel-member assignment for eligible FYP groups.\n•Availability management for panel members, supervisors, student groups, and\nrooms.\n•Defense date, time, room, or online-session scheduling.\n•Automatic detection of panel, supervisor, student, room, and conflict-of-interest\nclashes.\n•Optimized scheduling and rescheduling to reduce conflicts and balance panel work-\nload.\nPrimary Output: Conflict-free defense schedule and panel assignment.\nModule 9: Panel Evaluation & Feedback\nDescription: This module manages formal panel evaluation using configured rubrics.\nIt supports detailed feedback, AI-assisted defense preparation, evaluator-disagreement\ndetection, and traceable feedback resolution.\n•Criterion-based rubric scoring and recommendation by multiple panel members.\n•Paragraph, section, module/feature, and overall proposal/project comments.\n•AI-generated viva/defense questions based on proposal content and claimed contri-\nbution.\n•Detection of significant evaluator disagreement in scores or assessment dimensions.\n•Feedback-resolution workflow: Panel Comment→Coordinator Resolution→Stu-\ndent/Supervisor Revision→Panel Recheck, while preserving the original panel\ncomment.\nPrimary Output: Structured panel evaluation, feedback history, and resolution status.\nModule 10: Project Progress & Supervision Dashboard\nDescription: This module combines formal supervision evidence with milestone and\ntask progress to present the current health of an active FYP and identify delay or delivery\nrisks.\n[PAGE 13]\n•Supervision meeting records, minutes, decisions, action items, owners, and due\ndates.\n•AI extraction of tasks, owners, commitments, and deadlines from meeting notes.\n•Milestone, task, completion-percentage, overdue-work, and scope-progress tracking.\n•Project-health classification such as On Track, Attention Required, or Critical using\nrecorded evidence.\n•Risk explanation and recovery recommendations; when sufficient labelled data be-\ncomes available, predictive ML models and explainability can be added for delay\nprobability estimation.\nPrimary Output: Project Progress, Action Compliance, Risk, and Recovery Report.\nModule 11: Final FYP Archive & Handover\nDescription: This module creates the final institutional record of a completed FYP\nand prepares reusable knowledge for future students instead of treating the final report\nas a static file.\n•Final project submission package containing report, proposal, source-code reposi-\ntory, dataset/model information, and supporting files.\n•Automatic extraction of project metadata including domain, technologies, datasets,\nmodules, and methodology.\n•Classification of final scope as Completed, Partially Implemented, or Not Imple-\nmented.\n•Extraction of limitations, unresolved issues, technical debt, and future work.\n•Intelligent handover/continuation package containing reusable assets, dependencies,\nincomplete work, and recommended continuation points.\nPrimary Output: Final Project Archive Package and Continuation/Handover Package.\nModule 12: FYP Knowledge Search & Analytics\nDescription: This module reads and reasons over archived institutional FYP knowl-\nedge. It provides evidence-based natural-language search for authorized users and pro-\nduces institutional analytics without editing the underlying archive.\n•Natural-language semantic search over archived FYP titles, abstracts, modules,\ntechnologies, datasets, outcomes, and limitations.\n•RAG and GraphRAG-based question answering grounded in retrieved institutional\nevidence.\n•Multi-hop queries across relationships such as Project→Technology→Dataset→\nLimitation→Supervisor.\n•Source citations/evidence paths so generated answers can be traced to archived\nFYP records.\n[PAGE 14]\n•Institutional analytics for domain trends, supervisor workload, approval turnaround,\ncompletion/delay patterns, panel statistics, and technology usage over time.\nPrimary Output: Evidence-Based FYP Knowledge Answers and Institutional Analyt-\nics Report.\n10. Modules\nTable 2 provides the module distribution and task allocation according to the four\nimplementation checkpoints. The same pattern is used for each checkpoint: three modules\nare scheduled in P1 Mid, three in P1 Final, three in P2 Mid, and three in P2 Final.\nTable 2: FYPHub Module Distribution and Task Allocation\nDeadline Modules Student\nP1 Mid 1: Admin Dashboard & Academic Management Muhammad Maaz\n(23108179)\n2: Student Team & Collaboration Hub Zain Ali Kiani\n(23108197)\n3: Student Proposal & AI Compliance Check Danish Faraz (23108160)\nP1 Final 4: Proposal Similarity & Originality Check Muhammad Maaz\n(23108179)\n5: Supervisor Selection & Allocation Zain Ali Kiani\n(23108197)\n6: Supervisor Proposal Review & Revision Danish Faraz (23108160)\nP2 Mid 7: Coordinator Proposal Control Muhammad Maaz\n(23108179)\n8: Defense & Panel Scheduling Zain Ali Kiani\n(23108197)\n9: Panel Evaluation & Feedback Danish Faraz (23108160)\nP2 Final 10: Project Progress & Supervision Dashboard Muhammad Maaz\n(23108179)\n11: Final FYP Archive & Handover Zain Ali Kiani\n(23108197)\n12: FYP Knowledge Search & Analytics Danish Faraz (23108160)\n[PAGE 15]\n11. Development Environment\nTools: Visual Studio Code, Git/GitHub, PowerShell, Docker, Docker\nCompose, Swagger/OpenAPI, and Ollama for local AI model\nexecution.\nDBMS: PostgreSQL with the pgvector extension for transactional\nacademic data, proposal/version history, workflow records, archive\nmetadata, and semantic vector search.\nPlatform: Web-Based; Front-End: React + Vite + TypeScript with\nTailwind CSS; Back-End: Python 3.12 with FastAPI for\nauthenticated REST APIs, academic workflow rules, and AI\nservice orchestration.\nAI Envi-\nronment: \nLocal and/or approved language models through Ollama,\nembedding models, RAG/GraphRAG pipelines, Python-based AI\norchestration, machine-learning libraries for risk analysis, and\noptimization libraries for scheduling/allocation.\n12. Significance\nFYPHub is significant because it transforms the university’s FYP process from a\ncollection of disconnected administrative tasks into a connected academic intelligence\necosystem. The platform does not only digitize proposal forms; it links team formation,\nproposal quality, originality, supervision, coordinator routing, scheduling, panel evalua-\ntion, progress evidence, final archiving, and institutional knowledge reuse.\nThe main expected benefits are: \n•Improved Proposal Quality: Students receive structured feedback on university\nformatting, missing sections, academic consistency, and readiness before formal\nreview.\n•Reduced Duplication: Semantic and functional comparison against previous\nFYPs reveals overlap and clarifies where genuine new contribution should begin.\n•Better Team Formation: AI teammate matching and duplicate-membership\nrules support fair and compatible group creation.\n•Transparent Supervision: Supervisor selection considers expertise and capacity,\nwhile revision comments and meeting actions remain traceable.\n•Efficient Academic Routing: Coordinators can monitor proposal states, delays,\nand panel readiness from one controlled workflow.\n•Improved Defense Management: Panel assignments and defense schedules are\ngenerated with conflict and availability checks.\n•Evidence-Based Progress Monitoring: Meeting actions, milestones, tasks, and\ndelays contribute to project-health and recovery recommendations.\n•Institutional Knowledge Reuse: Final FYPs become searchable, reusable\nknowledge through RAG/GraphRAG and analytics rather than remaining isolated\ndocuments.\n[PAGE 16]\n•Human-Controlled AI: AI supports analysis, explanation, retrieval, prediction,\nand recommendations, while authorized academic personnel retain final decision\nauthority.\n13. Tools and Techniques\nFYPHub uses a modular web and AI architecture that separates deterministic aca-\ndemic rules from probabilistic AI assistance. The major tools and techniques are listed\nbelow.\n•Front-End: React + Vite + TypeScript with Tailwind CSS will provide responsive\nrole-specific interfaces for Student, Supervisor, Coordinator, Panel, and Adminis-\ntrator users [11].\n•Back-End: Python 3.12 with FastAPI will provide authenticated REST APIs, role-\nbased authorization, academic workflow rules, AI-service endpoints, and integration\nlogic [12].\n•Database: PostgreSQL will store users, teams, proposals, versions, assignments,\nreviews, coordinator actions, schedules, evaluations, progress records, and archive\nmetadata [13]. The pgvector extension will support proposal embeddings, semantic\nretrieval, and historical FYP similarity search [14].\n•AI and Intelligence: LLMs and embeddings will support proposal-quality anal-\nysis, semantic revision checking, teammate explanations, viva-question generation,\nsummarization, and semantic search. RAG will ground generated answers in re-\ntrieved institutional evidence [3], while GraphRAG will add graph-structured re-\ntrieval for broader and multi-hop reasoning across archived FYP relationships [4].\nOptimization techniques will support supervisor allocation and defense scheduling,\nwhile project-risk analysis will begin with deterministic/hybrid evidence and may\nlater use supervised ML when sufficient labelled data becomes available.\n•Security and Academic Rules: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) and de-\nterministic backend/database constraints will enforce group uniqueness, supervisor\nslots, proposal-version eligibility, coordinator routing, panel permissions, and ad-\nministrative policies rather than delegating these controls to an LLM.\n•Development Tools: Docker and Docker Compose will support reproducible\ninfrastructure [15]; Git/GitHub will support collaborative version control; Swag-\nger/OpenAPI will support API documentation and testing; Ollama will support\nlocal AI model execution.\nOverall Workflow: Admin Setup→Team Formation→Proposal Submission &\nAI Compliance→Similarity & Originality Check→Supervisor Selection & Allocation\n→Supervisor Review & Revision→Coordinator Proposal Control→Defense & Panel\nScheduling→Panel Evaluation & Feedback→Project Progress & Supervision→Final\nFYP Archive & Handover→FYP Knowledge Search & Analytics.\n[PAGE 17]\nReferences\n[1] R. Lounas, I. Hamzaoui, N. Bouguelmouna, and H. Mokrani, “An E-Collaboration\nApplication for Final-Year Project Management,”International Journal of e-\nCollaboration, vol. 19, no. 1, pp. 1–17, 2023. DOI: 10.4018/IJeC.315787.\n[2] A. S. Yusuf, “A Web-Based System for Final Year Student Project Allocation and\nManagement,”Journal of Basics and Applied Sciences Research, vol. 4, no. 3, pp.\n9–22, 2026. DOI: 10.4314/jobasr.v4i3.2.\n[3] P. Lewis et al., “Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP\nTasks,”Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2020. Avail-\nable: https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401.\n[4] D. Edge et al., “From Local to Global: A Graph RAG Approach to Query-Focused\nSummarization,” 2024. Available: https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16130.\n[5] Moodle, “Assignment Activity,”MoodleDocs. [Online]. Available: https://docs.\nmoodle.org/403/en/mod/assignment.\n[6] Turnitin, “Overview of the New Similarity Report Experience,”Turnitin\nGuides. [Online]. Available: https://guides.turnitin.com/hc/en-us/articles/\n24194876779661-Overview-of-the-new-Similarity-Report-experience.\n[7] OpenProject, “Project Collaboration Software Features,”OpenProject\nDocumentation. [Online]. Available: https://www.openproject.org/\ncollaboration-software-features/.\n[8] Microsoft, “Assignments and Grades in Your Class Team,”Microsoft Sup-\nport. [Online]. Available: https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/education/\nassignments/assignments-and-grades-in-your-class-team.\n[9] GitHub, “Planning and Tracking with Projects,”GitHub Docs.\n[Online]. Available: https://docs.github.com/en/issues/\nplanning-and-tracking-with-projects.\n[10] DSpace, “DSpace Features,”DSpace. [Online]. Available: https://dspace.org/\nfeatures/.\n[11] React, “React Documentation.” [Online]. Available: https://react.dev/.\n[12] FastAPI, “FastAPI Documentation.” [Online]. Available: https://fastapi.\ntiangolo.com/.\n[13] PostgreSQL Global Development Group, “PostgreSQL Documentation.” [Online].\nAvailable: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/.\n[14] pgvector, “Open-Source Vector Similarity Search for Postgres.” [Online]. Available: \nhttps: //github.com/pgvector/pgvector.\n[15] Docker, “Docker Documentation.” [Online]. Available: https://docs.docker.\ncom/.	{"scope": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "title": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "topic": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "purpose": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "warnings": ["topic was not detected in the uploaded document.", "objectives was not detected in the uploaded document.", "methodology was not detected in the uploaded document.", "scope was not detected in the uploaded document.", "proposed_solution was not detected in the uploaded document.", "expected_outcomes was not detected in the uploaded document."], "margin_mm": 31.59, "page_size": "A4", "objectives": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "page_count": 17, "font_family": "CMR12", "methodology": {"value": null, "source": null, "display": "Not detected in PDF", "source_page": null}, "table_count": 1, "figure_count": 1, "font_size_pt": 11.96, "line_spacing": 0.21, "top_margin_mm": 138.06, "other_sections": [{"value": "[PAGE 1]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Final Year Project Long Proposal", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "FYPHub: An Agentic AI-Driven", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Platform for Intelligent Final Year Project", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Management", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Muhammad Maaz 23108179", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Zain Ali Kiani 23108197", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Danish Faraz 23108160", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Supervised by: Sir Hamza Imran", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Department of Robotics & Artificial Intelligence", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Faculty of Computing and Engineering Sciences", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "SZABIST University", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Islamabad, Pakistan", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Fall 2026", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 2]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Revision History", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Compiled By Checked By Date Description V ersion", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Muhammad", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Maaz", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Sir Hamza Imran 07-Oct-2026 Long proposal structure", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and content", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "1.0", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Zain Ali Kiani Sir Hamza Imran 07-Oct-2026 Module and feature", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "review", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "1.1", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Danish Faraz Sir Hamza Imran 07-Oct-2026 Workflow and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "technology review", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "1.2", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Project Team Sir Hamza Imran 07-Oct-2026 Final 12-module draft 1.3", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 3]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "1. Project Stream", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "✓ Web-Based FYP Desktop Application", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Mobile Application Game", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Hardware-Based", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "2. Introduction", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Final Year Project (FYP) management is a multi-stage academic process involving", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "proposal processing, project allocation, collaboration, project completion, evaluation,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and administrative coordination. Prior FYP-management research has shown that these", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "activities benefit from centralized digital workflows and role-specific collaboration tools", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "rather than disconnected manual processes [1, 2]. In many university environments,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "forms, spreadsheets, emails, messaging applications, and loosely organized digital records", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "can make proposal tracking, supervisor coordination, evaluation, and reuse of previous", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "project knowledge difficult.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "FYPHubis proposed as a centralized, role-aware, AI-assisted platform for managing", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "the complete FYP lifecycle. The platform is designed for five major roles: Student, Super-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "visor, FYP Coordinator, Panel Member, and Administrator. It combines deterministic", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "academic rules with Large Language Models (LLMs), semantic embeddings, Retrieval-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Augmented Generation (RAG), GraphRAG, machine-learning based risk analysis, opti-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "mization techniques, and agentic AI orchestration. RAG provides language models with", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "retrieved external evidence for knowledge-intensive generation, while GraphRAG extends", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "retrieval with graph-structured relationships for broader and multi-hop reasoning over", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "document collections [3, 4]. AI is used for assistance, analysis, retrieval, explanation, and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "prediction, while final academic authority remains with authorized university personnel.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The FYP is organized into twelve clearly separated modules. Each module owns a", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "distinct part of the FYP lifecycle so that proposal management, quality compliance, orig-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "inality checking, supervision, scheduling, evaluation, progress monitoring, institutional", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "knowledge retrieval, and final archiving do not overlap unnecessarily.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "3. Project Description", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "FYPHub is a responsive web-based platform that digitizes and intelligently connects", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "the complete Final Year Project process. Students can form groups, find compatible", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "teammates, create and submit proposal versions, run AI-assisted compliance checks, com-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "pare proposals against previous FYPs, and request suitable supervisors. Supervisors can", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "review proposals using structured comments and revision requests, while the Coordina-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tor manages academic routing and panel readiness. The system also schedules defenses,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "supports structured panel evaluation, tracks project progress and supervision evidence,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "archives completed FYPs, and makes institutional project knowledge searchable through", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "RAG and GraphRAG.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The platform follows a shared-module, role-based-access architecture. A feature is not", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "duplicated into separate modules merely because different roles can view it. For example,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "the proposal compliance engine remains one module, but students, supervisors, coordina-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tors, and panel members access different views according to their permissions. Similarly,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "the final archive creates and preserves institutional knowledge, while the knowledge-search", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "module retrieves and reasons over that archived information.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The system is expected to provide a single source of truth for proposal versions,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 4]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "supervisor assignments, review comments, coordinator actions, panel evaluations, project", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "progress, and final project outcomes. This improves traceability and supports data-driven", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "academic decision making across the entire FYP lifecycle.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "4. Real World Problem", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Universities generate a large amount of valuable FYP knowledge every semester, but", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "that knowledge is often difficult to search, compare, and reuse. Students may not know", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "whether an idea has already been implemented, what limitations previous projects had,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "which supervisor is most suitable, or which university formatting and proposal require-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ments are still unmet. Group formation can also be difficult when students do not have", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "suitable teammates, and duplicate membership can occur if group rules are not centrally", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "enforced.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "FYP administration also involves several academic actors. Students submit propos-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "als, supervisors review them, coordinators control academic routing, panels evaluate the", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "work, and administrators configure academic rules and deadlines. When these activities", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "are distributed across disconnected tools, important context is lost. Revision comments", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "can become scattered, supervisor capacity may be unclear, panel scheduling can create", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "conflicts, progress evidence may remain informal, and completed projects can become", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "static files rather than reusable institutional knowledge.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The real-world challenge is therefore broader than simple proposal submission. The", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "university needs an integrated system that manages the workflow, applies deterministic", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "academic rules, provides AI-assisted analysis where appropriate, preserves an auditable", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "history, and converts historical FYP records into an active knowledge base for future", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "students and faculty.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "5. Problem Statement", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Current FYP management practices may suffer from fragmented team formation,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "manual proposal submission, inconsistent formatting, weak originality checks, limited", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "visibility of supervisor expertise and capacity, scattered review communication, man-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ual defense scheduling, disconnected panel feedback, limited progress evidence, and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "poor reuse of completed-project knowledge. Existing generic learning-management,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "plagiarism-detection, and project-management tools can support individual parts of this", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "process, but they do not provide a unified FYP-specific platform that connects academic", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "workflow, proposal intelligence, supervisor allocation, panel evaluation, project progress,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and institutional knowledge.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The proposed project aims to develop theFYPHubplatform, which manages the", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "complete FYP lifecycle through twelve integrated but non-overlapping modules. The", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "system will combine role-based workflow control with AI-assisted proposal compliance,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "semantic originality analysis, supervisor matching, revision verification, scheduling opti-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "mization, progress and risk intelligence, RAG-based institutional knowledge search, and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "final project handover support.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "6. Names of Existing Similar FYPs (if repeated)", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "No exact repeated FYP has been confirmed in the current project records at proposal", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "stage. However, the problem space overlaps partially with learning-management systems,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "plagiarism/similarity platforms, project-management tools, and institutional repositories.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The proposed FYPHub differs by integrating the complete FYP lifecycle and by using", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "archived institutional FYP data as an active source for similarity analysis, knowledge", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "retrieval, extension guidance, and analytics.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 5]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The proposed system will also contain its ownProposal Similarity & Originality", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Checkmodule. Once historical FYP records are available in the repository, this module", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "will compare a new proposal against previous projects and provide evidence of semantic,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "functional, and methodological overlap rather than relying only on title similarity.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "7. New Functionalities (main) to be added Features", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "FYPHub introduces several capabilities that are not normally available together in a", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "single FYP management platform: ", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•AI-assisted teammate discovery using compatibility questions, skills, availability,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "work style, and role preferences, with duplicate-group membership prevention.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•University-template-aware proposal compliance checking for structure, headings,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "margins, spacing, alignment, required sections, and academic consistency.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Semantic and functional comparison of new proposals against archived FYPs, in-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "cluding contribution boundaries and extension gaps.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Expertise, workload, preference, and capacity-based supervisor recommendation", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and allocation with slot enforcement.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Structured supervisor comments, revision requests, two-way discussion, and AI-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "assisted verification of whether requested revisions were resolved.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Coordinator-controlled academic routing with delay and workflow-conformance", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "monitoring.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Conflict-aware defense scheduling and panel assignment based on availability,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "room/time constraints, and academic conflicts.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Panel scoring and feedback with AI-generated viva questions, evaluator disagree-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ment detection, and feedback-resolution tracking.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Supervision-meeting evidence, milestone tracking, project-health classification,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "delay-risk analysis, and recovery recommendations.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Final FYP archiving with implemented-scope extraction, limitations, future work,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "reusable assets, and intelligent handover packages.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•RAG/GraphRAG-based institutional FYP search with evidence and multi-hop rea-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "soning over archived projects.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Institutional analytics for domain trends, workload, turnaround time, completion", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "patterns, and management insights.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8. Application Review", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "This section reviews six existing platforms that address selected parts of the FYP", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "lifecycle. Each application is summarized through two key characteristics and two limita-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tions, followed by a feature-by-application comparison table in the same tabular pattern", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "used throughout this proposal. The purpose is to show which capabilities already exist", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "in established systems and which FYP-specific gaps remain.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 6]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.1 Moodle Assignment Activity [5]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Moodle provides structured assignment submission, group submissions, deadlines,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "grading, feedback, and rubric-based assessment within a learning-management environ-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ment.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 1: Students can submit files or online text individually or in", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "groups, with configurable availability and due dates.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 2: Instructors can grade submissions, provide feedback, and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "use rubrics or custom grading scales.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of Moodle for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 1: It does not provide FYP-specific supervisor expertise/capacity", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "matching, allocation optimization, or defense-panel scheduling.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 2: It does not provide institutional FYP semantic originality analysis,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "GraphRAG reasoning, or intelligent handover/continuation support.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.2 Turnitin Similarity Report [6]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Turnitin compares submitted text against large source collections and produces a Sim-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ilarity Report that highlights matching text, source information, and an overall similarity", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "percentage.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 1: Text matching against internet sources, publications, and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "previously submitted work with source-level evidence.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 2: Similarity reports support academic review by grouping", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "and presenting matched passages for human interpretation.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of Turnitin for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 1: It primarily measures textual similarity and does not evaluate FYP", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "problem, objectives, modules/features, methodology, implemented scope, and con-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tribution boundaries as one semantic project comparison.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 2: It does not manage FYP team formation, supervisor allocation,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "coordinator routing, defense scheduling, project-progress intelligence, or final han-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "dover.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.3 OpenProject [7]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "OpenProject is a general project-management and collaboration platform supporting", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "planning, tasks, milestones, workloads, timelines, and project progress.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 1: Work packages, tasks, milestones, timelines, and responsi-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "bilities can be planned and tracked across a project lifecycle.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 2: Team collaboration, workload planning, project dash-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "boards, and status tracking support coordinated project delivery.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of OpenProject for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 1: It does not provide university-template proposal compliance, histor-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ical FYP originality intelligence, or formal supervisor revision-compliance checking.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 2: It does not provide FYP-specific supervisor allocation, panel evalu-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ation, institutional FYP archive reasoning, or GraphRAG-based knowledge search.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 7]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.4 Microsoft Teams for Education [8]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Microsoft Teams for Education combines class communication, channels, assignments,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "due dates, resources, feedback, and rubric-based grading in one collaborative environ-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ment.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 1: Channels and class-team communication support collabo-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "ration among students and educators.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 2: Assignments support due dates, file submission, resubmis-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "sion, resources, grades, and rubrics.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of Microsoft Teams for Education for the target FYP lifecycle are listed", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 1: It does not provide FYP-specific teammate compatibility matching,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "supervisor expertise/capacity allocation, or historical FYP originality analysis.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 2: It does not manage coordinator academic routing, defense-panel", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "optimization, project handover intelligence, or RAG/GraphRAG reasoning over", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "FYP archives.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.5 GitHub Projects [9]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "GitHub Projects supports software-team planning and tracking through issues, pull", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "requests, project boards, tables, roadmaps, custom fields, and code-linked work items.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 1: Issues and Projects can organize tasks, ideas, bugs, mile-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "stones, and development work with configurable views and metadata.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 2: Work tracking is closely linked with repositories, pull re-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "quests, and software-development activity.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of GitHub Projects for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 1: It is development-work oriented and does not provide university", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "proposal formatting/compliance, supervisor matching, or formal academic routing.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 2: It does not provide defense scheduling, panel rubric evaluation,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "semantic comparison with archived FYPs, or institutional FYP handover workflows.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.6 DSpace Institutional Repository [10]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "DSpace is an institutional repository platform for managing, preserving, controlling", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "access to, and searching digital content and metadata.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 1: It preserves multiple digital formats and supports", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "metadata/full-text search for institutional collections.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 2: Granular access control and repository organization sup-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "port long-term institutional knowledge preservation.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "The limitations of DSpace for the target FYP lifecycle are listed below.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 1: It is primarily a repository and does not manage active FYP team", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "formation, proposal review, supervisor allocation, progress, or defense workflows.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Limitation 2: Standard repository search does not provide FYP-specific seman-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "tic originality analysis, revision verification, or RAG/GraphRAG-based multi-hop", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "academic reasoning by itself.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "[PAGE 8]", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "8.7 FYPHub (Our FYP)", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "FYPHub combines the complete university FYP lifecycle in one role-aware platform", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "instead of treating submission, similarity checking, supervision, evaluation, progress, and", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "institutional knowledge as disconnected activities.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 1: Integrated 12-module workflow covering team formation,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "proposal compliance/originality, supervisor allocation/revision, coordinator control,", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "defense/panel processes, project progress, final archive, and handover.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "•Key characteristic 2: AI-assisted institutional intelligence using LLMs, embed-", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "dings, RAG/GraphRAG, optimization, and evidence-based analysis while retaining", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "human academic authority.", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "Table 1: Applications Comparison", "source": "page 1", "source_page": 1}, {"value": "F eature Moodle[